Members of the Welsh assembly concerned that they “identify them as Muppets”
Several members of the assembly have expressed their dismayal at the proposal to modify their title by MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
This controversy has arisen as a result of the plan to change the name of the assembly to that of the Welsh Parliament.
The members of the assembly of all the different political parties have shown their concern that this measure may become a cause for mockery.
A member of the Labour party assembly has stated that his party was concerned that MWP “rhymed with Twp or Pwp”.
For non-Welsh readers: In Welsh, twp means stupid and, pwp, excrement.
A member of Plaid Cymru’s party assembly has commented that his party “don’t see it with good eyes” and has proposed several alternatives.
For his part, a Welsh conservative member has stated that his group was “open” in the face of the name change, but also commented that there is too small a difference between the term “MWP” and “Muppet”.
In this context, it must be said that the letter “w” in Welsh is pronounced similarly to the letter “one” in English from Yorkyshire.
According to the committee of the assembly in charge of elaborating the legal draft to introduce the name changes: “The final decision on any qualifier to name the members of the assembly will fall of course on the members themselves.”
The Wales Government's 2017 Act grants the Welsh Assembly all the power to change its name.
In June, the committee published the results of a public consultation on the proposals, with broad support for the proposal to change the name of the assembly to that of the Welsh Parliament.
Regarding the title of the members of the assembly, although the committee opted for “Members of the Welsh parliament” or “WPM”, the “MWP” option is the one that received the widest support for public consultation.
Apparently, the members of the assembly are suggesting different alternatives. However, the attempt to reach a consensus can be a headache for the President of the assembly, Elin Jones, who is expected to present the legal draft on the changes in a matter of weeks.
Legislation on reforms will include other changes related to the operation of the assembly, such as rules on the disqualification of its members and the designing of the committee system.
The members of the assembly will hold the final vote on their name during the debate related to the legislation.
The Macedonians will vote in a referendum on their country's name change
Citizens will choose on Sunday whether or not they want to change the name of their country to “Republic of North Macedonia”.
The popular consultation was decided in an attempt to resolve a dispute that has lasted decades with the neighboring country Greece, which already has a province called Macedonia.
Athens has insisted for quite some time on the fact that the name of its northern neighbor represents a way of claiming its territory and has repeatedly opposed this country's attempts to be part of the EU and NATO.
The Macedonian president, Gjorge Ivanov, against the plebiscite on the change of name, has stated that he will ignore the vote.
However, those in favor, such as Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, believe that the name change is simply the price that must be paid to become a member of the EU and NATO.
The bells of St. Martin's stop ringing: the difficulties of Harlem's churches
"Historically, the seniors I've spoken to say there was a bar and a church on every corner," he said.
“Today, there’s none of them left.”
According to Adams, the disappearance of bars can be understood.
"People have another way of socializing today," he says.
“Bars have already ceased to be that kind of living rooms for the neighborhood where people went periodically.”
As for the churches, he is concerned that the money obtained from the sale of assets does not last as long as that foreseen by their leaders “and that sooner or later they will return to the place where they came from.”
The churches, he adds, could end up being replaced by apartment buildings and condominiums with the kind of people who won't be out of any help for the temples that remain in the neighborhood.
“The vast majority of people who buy condominiums in these buildings will be white,” he says, “and therefore what they will do will accelerate the closure of all the churches, since it is quite unlikely that the majority of people who move towards these condominiums will end up being members of these churches.”
Both churches were built by white congregations before Harlem became a black metropolis; Metropolitan Communicity church in 1870 and St. Martin's ten years later.
The white Methodist congregation that was there originally marched during the 30's.
A nearby black congregation acquired ownership of the building.
St. Martin's passed into the hands of a reverend-led black congregation John Howard Johnson, who led a boycott of 125th street retail merchants, one of Harlem's main shopping streets, who resisted hiring or promoting black people.
In 1939, a fire caused serious damage to the building, and whether the parishioners of the Reverend Johnson made plans to rebuild it, it was then that they commissioned the carrilló.
Reverend David Johnson, son of Father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin's Church, proudly described the carrilló as "the bells of poor people."
The expert who sounded the carrilló in July described it differently: “A cultural treasure” and an “irreplaceable historical instrument”.
The expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also observed that it was the world's first rail that was played by a black-race musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who had to move to Riverside Church's largest rail 18 years ago.
According to Mr Merriweather, St. Martin's did not replace him.
Which has sounded in St. Martin's in recent months, on the other hand, have been the complicated stories of architects and contractors, some of them brought by the lay leaders of the church and others by the episcopal diocese.
The council, the governing body of the parish, made up of lay leaders, wrote to the diocese last July expressing its concern that the diocese “intended to transmit the costs” to the council, although the latter was not involved in the hiring of the architects and contractors that the diocese sent.
Some parishioners have complained about the lack of transparency on the part of the diocese.
Shark injures 13-year-old boy while looking for lobsters in California
A shark attacked and injured a 13-year-old boy on Saturday while taking a dive to look for lobsters in California on the first day of lobster season, authorities said.
The attack took place just before 7 a.m. near Beacon's Beach at Encinitas.
According to Chad Hammel in the KSWB-TV in San Diego, he had spent half an hour diving with some friends on Saturday morning when he heard the screams of a boy asking for help. Then they paddled with a group to the place where the boy was to help him get him out of the water.
Hammel says he first thought it was about the joy of having caught a lobster, but then “realized that he was screaming 'I've been bitten!
I've been bitten!'
He had the whole collarbone torn," Hammel observed when he reached the boy.
"For everyone to get out of the water, I shouted: 'There's a shark in the water!'" Hammel adds.
The boy was transported by air at Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego, where he was admitted in serious condition.
It is not known what is the species of shark responsible for the attack.
In a press conference, lifeguard Larry Giles commented that work a few weeks that a shark had been detected in the area, but that it was decided that this species was not dangerous.
Giles added that the victim suffered traumatic injuries in the upper area of the torso.
Authorities closed for 48 hours access to beaches from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's in Ecinitas because of the investigation and for safety reasons.
Giles remarked that there are more than 135 species of shark in the area, but that most of them are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plans, towards the UK beauty products market
Sainsbury's has decided to compete in Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams with hallways of beauty products in the style of occupied department stores with specialized employees.
These great corridors are only part of a considerable investment in the market of beauty products in the United Kingdom, a market that moves about 2800 million GBP and that continues to grow while the sectors of fashion and household products are left behind, and will be tested in 11 stores around the country to expand to more stores next year if the test becomes a success.
The investment in beauty products is the product of the greed of supermarkets to take advantage of the space on their shelves that was previously intended for televisions, microwaves and household products.
Sainsbury's claims it will double its beauty offering to offer up to 3000 different products, first including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
Existing ranges from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees will also receive more space with brand areas similar to those that can be found in stores such as Boots.
The supermarket company is also in the relaunch phase of its range of Boutique make-up products so that most of the products are now suitable for vegans, an aspect increasingly demanded by younger consumers.
The retail chain The Fragrance Shop will also open concessions to two Sainsbury's stores. The first did so last week in Croydon, south London, while the second will open in Selly Oak, Birmingham, later this year.
Online shopping and the shift towards a habit of buying food in smaller quantities every day in small supermarkets has made large supermarkets have to find other ways to convince people to come.
According to Mike Coupe, chief executive of Sainsbury's, stores will increasingly look like department stores in a supermarket chain's attempt to battle Aldi and Lidl discount supermarkets with more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury's has been introducing Argos outlets in many of its supermarkets, in addition to a number of Habitat stores since it acquired both chains two years ago, which, he says, has served to relaunch grocery sales and increase the profitability of acquisitions.
Previous supermarket chain's earlier attempt to relaunch its departments of beauty products and pharmacy resulted in a failure.
Sainsbury's did a joint operation test with Boots in the early 2000s, but it all ended hastily by not agreeing on how to divide pharmacy chain's profits inside supermarkets.
This new strategy comes after the sale three years ago by Sainsbury's of its 281 pharmacies business in Celesio, owner of LloydsPharmacy chain, for £125 million.
According to Sainsbury's, Lloyds will play an important role in the global plan, as it will expand the range of luxury brands of leather products, such as La Roche-Posay or Vichy, to four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, commercial director of Sainsbury's, comments: "We have transformed the appearance of our beauty product corridors in order to improve the environment for our customers.
We have invested in specifically trained workers who will be of great help when it comes to providing advice.
Our range of brands is designed to adapt to any need; on the other hand, the attractive environment and the practical location mean that we are now one of the favorite destinations for beauty products and that we defy the traditional way of shopping.”
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willoughby backs GBP £11 million deal
Dragons' Den star Peter Jones left "furious" after TV presenter Holly Willoughby backtracked £££11million deal with Jones' lifestyle brand business to focus on her new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV.
Willoughby doesn't have time to devote to business set household products and Truly accessories.
The business of these two media characters had compared with Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
This Morning presenter, 37, used Instagram to announce her departure.
Holly Willoughby has left Dragons' star Den Peter Jones enraged by retiring at the last minute of the lucratious joint lifestyle business to focus on his new contracts of his own with Marks & Spencer and ITV, which will also bring him great profits.
According to various sources, Jones was “furious” when the blonde television star admitted, during a tense meeting held on Tuesday at the headquarters of her empire in Marlow, Buckinghamshire, that the presenter’s new agreements, worth £1.5 million, implied that she would no longer have enough time to dedicate to the joint brand of household products and Truly accessories.
The business had compared with Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand and aspired to double Willoughby's fortune, estimated at £11 million.
When Willoughby, 37, used Instagram to announce Truly's departure, Jones took a private jet heading towards one of his holiday residences outside the UK.
“Truly was Holly’s top priority by far.
It was meant to be its long-term future and where we would see it for the next twenty or thirty years.
His decision to back down left all parties involved speechless.
No one could believe what happened on Tuesday, we were so close to launch...
Right now there is a warehouse full of goods in Marlow’s headquarters ready to be sold.”
According to experts, the departure of the presenter of This Morning, one of the UK's most profitable stars, could cost the company millions due to the significant investment that had been made in products, from pillows and candles to clothing and household clothing, and the possibility of delays for its launch.
In addition, it could mean the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby, a mother of three children, and her husband Dan Baldwin had maintained an intimate relationship with Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby founded Truly along with Capp in 2016, and 52-year-old Jones joined him occupying the position of president last March.
Both couples spend the holidays together. In addition, Jones owns 40 % stakes in Baldwin's television production company.
Willoughby will be M&S's upcoming brand ambassador and will replace Ant McPartlin as host of ITV's I'm A Celebrity program.
A source close to Jones commented last night that “We won’t make any comments about their businesses.”
Hard accusations ... “and then we fell in love”
He joked about the possible criticism he would receive from the media to make a comment that some would consider “little typical of a president” and to have such a positive opinion of the North Korean leader.
Why has Trump given up so much?
Trump claimed, while making fun of a news presenter's tone of voice:
“I didn’t give up for anything.”
He observed that Kim had an interest in holding a second meeting after the first held in Singapore in June was declared as a major step towards de-nuclearization of North Korea.
Negotiations towards denuclearization are stagnant.
More than three months after the summit held in June in Singapore, senior North Korean diplomat Ri Yong Ho commented on Saturday to the leaders of the United Nations General Assembly that North Korea does not see a “response in correspondence” by the U.S. to the first disarmament initiatives carried out by his country.
However, he observed, the U.S. continues to apply sanctions in order to maintain pressure.
Trump's view, on the other hand, was much more optimistic in his speech.
"We're doing very well with North Korea," he said.
“We were going to declare war on North Korea.
Millions of people would have lost their lives.
And now we have that great relationship.”
According to Trump, his efforts to improve relations with Kim have had positive results: end of missile tests, help to free hostages and return home from the remains of American soldiers.
And he defended his unusual approach by talking about relationships with Kim.
"It's very easy to be presidential, but instead of having 10 000 people trying to get into this venue full of people, we'd have about 200," Trump said.
An island destroyed and hundreds dead due to a tsunami and an earthquake in Indonesia
After the Lombok earthquake, for example, non-governmental organizations from other countries were told that they weren't necessary.
Although more than 10% of the population of Lombok had to be displaced, no type of national disaster was declared, a prerequisite to be able to catalyze international aid.
"Unfortunately, in many cases, they have made it very clear that they do not ask for international assistance, so the situation is quite demanding," says Ms. Sumbung.
While Save the Children is organizing a team to go to Palu, it's not safe even if foreign people can work on the ground.
Mr. Sutopo, a spokesperson for the national disaster agency, commented that the Indonesian authorities were assessing the situation in Palu to see whether or not international agencies are allowed to contribute when it comes to offering aid.
Given the constant quakes that Indonesia suffers, the country unfortunately remains unprepared for the wrath of nature.
Although shelters have been built in Aceh in case of tsunami, they are not frequent on other coasts.
The apparent lack of alert sirens in Palu in the event of a tsunami, even if there was a warning in force, is very likely to have contributed to the death toll.
Moving between the huge number of Indonesian islands is, at best, a challenge.
And natural disasters make logistics even more difficult.
A hospital ship that was docked in Lombok to care for the victims of the earthquake is now heading towards Palu; however, it will take at least three days to reach the site of the new disaster.
President Joko Widodo had as the central element of his election campaign the improvement of Indonesia's damaged infrastructure, and has invested huge amounts of money on roads and railroad tracks.
However, Joko's government is facing re-election loaded with funding deficits next year.
Joko is also facing pressure from the country's permanent sectarian tensions, where members of the Muslim majority have adopted a more conservative faith.
More than 1000 dead and tens of thousands of displaced people are the result of fighting taking place in the streets between Christian and Muslim gangs with machetes, bows and arrows, and other bloodthirsty weapons.
Notes Daniel Sturridge of Liverpool scores the goal of the draw with a distant shot against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool from a defeat in the Premier League against Chelsea with his goal scored in the 89th minute on Saturday at Stamford Bridge, London.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri about 27 meters from Chelsea's goal when his team was losing 1 to 0.
He accommodated the ball to the left before letting go of an unbelieving shot aimed at the second post.
The shot crossed the entire area with a large elevation as it propelled to the right crossing of the goal.
The ball finally started the descent above a Kepa Arrizabalaga in full jump to try to stop it and ended at the bottom of the net.
"I was only trying to reach that position to be able to shoot, and players like Shaqiri always play it forward as much as possible, so I tried to create myself as long as possible," Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
“I saw Kanté coming, I made a touch and I didn’t think much about it, I just dedicated myself to kicking.”
Chelsea won 1-0 at the break after scoring a goal in the 25th minute through their Belgian star Eden Hazard.
On this move, the Blue team striker returned from heel a pass to Mateo Kovacic before starting from almost midfield and sprinting towards Liverpool's field.
Kovacic made a quick combination from midfield.
He then made a superb pass in depth, which left Hazard inside the area with the ball controlled.
Hazard overcame the defense to finish at the second post with a shot with his left leg where Liverpool goalkeeper Alisson Becker couldn't get there.
Liverpool will face Napoli in the Champions League group stage next Wednesday at 15:00 at Stadio San Paolo in Napolis, Italy.
Chelsea, meanwhile, will play with Videoton in the UEFA Europa League on Thursday at 15:00 in London.
The death toll from the Indonesian tsunami reaches 832
The death toll as a result of the earthquake and tsunami has risen to 832, according to data from the country's disaster agency reported on Sunday morning.
Many people were trapped among the debris of collapsed buildings due to the 7.5 magnitude earthquake that took place on Friday and that caused waves of up to 6 meters, according to Sutopo spokesman Purwo Nugroho at a press conference.
The city of Palu, with more than 380 000 inhabitants, was completely covered by the rubble of destroyed buildings.
Police arrest 32-year-old man as murder suspect after finding a stabbed woman
The murder investigation has begun after the woman's body was found this morning in Birkenhead, Merseyside.
The 44-year-old woman was found at 7:55 a.m. with several stabbings at Grayson Mews, John Street; a 32-year-old man was then arrested as a murder suspect.
The police have asked the inhabitants of the area to have seen or heard something in relation to the crime to appear before the authorities.
“While the investigation is still in a preliminary phase, I would like to appeal to everyone who is close to John Street in Birkenhead and to have seen or heard anything suspicious, please contact us.
I’d also like to appeal to anyone, especially taxi drivers who’ve been able to capture anything through the car’s on-border camera, to get in touch, as they might have vital information for research.”
A police spokesman has confirmed that the woman whose body they found is from Birkenhead and that he was found inside a property.
This afternoon, friends who believe they know the woman have arrived at the crime scene to ask questions about where he was found this morning.
While the investigations continue, the police have commented that they are reporting the facts to relatives closest to the victim.
A taxi driver who lives in Grayson Mews has barely tried to return to his flat, but police have informed him that no one is allowed to enter or leave the building.
When he knew what had happened, he was speechless.
Residents are being warned to spend hours until they can re-enter.
It could have been heard as a police officer told a man that the entire area is treated as at the crime scene.
A woman appeared on the scene crying.
“It’s terrible!”
At 14:00, two police vans were inside the cordoned area with another van parked outside.
A number of agents remained within the cordoned area controlling the block of flats.
Anyone who can have information is asked to send a message directly to @MerPolCC, call 101 or contact Crimestoppers anonymously through 0800 555 111 and mentioning file 247 of September 30.
Cromwell statue of the British parliament becomes the last monument affected by the discussion about “rewriting history”
His disappearance would be a kind of poetic justice for the destruction in the purest style of the Taliban of so many cultural and religious objects of England perpetrated by his fanatical Puritan followers.
However, the Cromwell Society has described Crick’s proposal as “madness” and an “attempt to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, comments: “In today’s debate around the withdrawal of statues, it was inevitable that Oliver Cromwell in front of the Palace of Westminster would become one of the main objectives.
The iconoclasmy of the English civil wars was not ordered or carried out by Cromwell.
Perhaps he would end up sacrificing the incorrect Cromwell for the actions of his ancestor that he did during the previous century.
Cromwell's magnificent depiction by Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft is proof of 19th-century opinion and part of the historiography of a figure that many still believe is worthy of being commemorated.
Goldsmith told The Sunday Telegraph: “Cromwell is considered by many, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than now, as a supporter of parliament in the face of external pressure, in his case obviously of the monarchy.
Whether or not this fact constitutes a totally accurate representation remains a subject of historical debate.
What is certain is that the conflict of the mid-17th century shaped the subsequent development of our nation, and Cromwell is a recognizable figure who represents a side of this division.
His achievements as Lord Protector are also worthy to be celebrated and commemorated.”
Murderer pig kills Chinese farmer
A farmer was attacked and killed by a pig at a market in south-western China, local sources reported.
The man, identified only by his surname “Yuan”, was found dead with an artery cut and all covered with blood near a pig court at Liupanshui market in Guizhu province, the South China Morning Post reported on Sunday.
On May 30, 2005, a pig farmer was preparing to inject vaccines to pigs at a court in Xining, Qinghai Province, China.
Apparently, he had traveled Wednesday with his cousin from the neighboring province of Yunnan to sell 15 pigs to the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and uncovered an open door overlooking an adjoining pig court.
According to what he claimed, in the court there was a large male pig with blood in his mouth.
The forensic examination confirmed that the 250-kg piglet had death wounded the farmer, according to the report.
"My cousin had his legs shattered and full of blood," said the cousin, surnamed "Wu," according to Guiyang's evening news.
Scenes recorded by the security camera show how Yuan enters the market at 4:40 on Thursday morning to feed the pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man was not owned either by Yuan or his cousin.
One of those responsible for the market commented in the evening news that the pig remained closed and isolated in order to prevent him from attacking anyone else, while the police gathered the evidence at the scene of the crime.
Reportedly, Yuan's family and market managers are negotiating compensation for his death.
Despite being very rare, there have been previously cases of pigs that attack humans.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on their farm in Massachusetts, and left the man with serious injuries.
Ten years earlier, an almost 300 kg pig caught a Welsh farmer against his tractor until his wife was able to scare the animal.
After an Oregon farmer was devoured by his pigs in 2012, a farmer from Manitoba told CBC news that pigs are not normally aggressive but that the taste of blood can act as “trigger.”
"They like to play.
They're like children, they're very curious... they don't go with the idea of hurting.
You just have to give them the respect they deserve," he said.
Remains of Hurricane Rosa will bring torrential rains across south-western U.S
According to forecasts, Hurricane Rosa is weakening as it progresses over the coldest waters off the northern coast of Mexico.
However, Rosa will bring torrential rains throughout northern Mexico and southwestern U.S. over the next few days.
El Rosa had winds of 138 km/h, typical of a Category 1 hurricane, at 5:00 a.m. on Sunday morning, U.S. Eastern time, and was located 620 km southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move in a northerly direction on Sunday.
In the meantime, a depression is beginning to form in the Pacific Ocean, which is shifting east towards the U.S. west coast. When the Rose approaches the Baja California Peninsula on Monday as a tropical storm, it will begin to leave strong tropical moisture in a northerly direction to the south-western U.S.
El Rosa will leave up to 250 mm of rain in some parts of Mexico on Monday.
Next, tropical humidity will interact with the depression that is approaching to generate widespread torrential rains throughout the southwest over the next few days.
At various points, 25 to 100 mm of rain will cause dangerous floods, waste flows and the possibility of landslides in the desert.
The strong tropical humidity will cause precipitation rates close to 50 to 75 mm per hour at various points, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
50 to 100 mm of rain is expected in parts of the south-west, especially in much of Arizona.
There is a possibility that floods will form, since the situation is worsening very quickly due to the dispersed character of tropical rains.
It is completely discouraged to enter the desert on foot due to the threat of tropical rain.
Torrential rains could cause gorges to become uncontrolled rivers. In addition, storms can bring gusts of wind and dust clouds at specific points.
The approaching depression will bring torrential rains at certain points off the coast of Southern California.
It is possible that totals of precipitation above 50 mm are recorded, which could lead to minor waste flows and slippery paths.
It would be the first rainfall of the rainy season in the region.
Isolated tropical rain downpours will begin to arrive in Arizona between Sunday night and Monday morning, before the rain becomes more widespread between Monday night and Tuesday.
Torrential rains will spread into the Four Corners region on Tuesday and will persist throughout Wednesday.
In October there will be several significant temperature fluctuations throughout the U.S. as the Arctic cools, although the tropics will remain quite warm.
This phenomenon can sometimes cause drastic changes in temperature in a short distance.
U.S. central region on Sunday is a clear example of drastic temperature differences.
There have been temperature differences of nearly 20 degrees between Kansas City, Missouri, and Omaha, Nebraska, and between St. Louis and Des Moines, Iowa.
Over the next few days, persistent summer heat will try to expand again.
It is expected to start in October with heat in much of central and eastern U.S., with temperatures around 27-degrees from the southern Plains area to parts of the north-east.
New York City could reach 27 degrees on Tuesday, which would represent about £10 above average.
According to the long-term weather forecast, there are many chances that above-average temperatures will be recorded in the eastern U.S. during the first half of October.
More than 20 million viewers watched Brett Kavanaugh's statement
More than 20 million viewers on Thursday watched the thrilling testimony of Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh and the woman who accused him of sexual abuse, which allegedly took place in the 80's, Christine Blasey Ford. The testimony was broadcast to six different television networks.
In the meantime, the political impasse continued, with broadcasters disrupting Friday's usual programming due to a turnarount of events at the last minute: an agreement designed by Arizona Senator Jeff Flake for the FBI to conduct an investigation of charges for a week.
Ford explained to the Senate Committee for judicial matters that she was absolutely convinced that Kavanaugh touched her drunk and attempted to take off her clothes at a high school party.
Kavanaugh, in an exalted witness, stated that he was absolutely convinced that this never happened.
According to Nielsen, more than 20.4 million viewers are likely to have seen this testimony on Friday.
The company accounted for average viewers of CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
Audience figures were not immediately available from other broadcasters that forwarded testimony, such as PBS, C-SPAN or Fox Business Network.
In addition, Nielsen usually has trouble calculatinging people who see it from their office.
Put into perspective, it is an audience similar to that of an American football playoff game or that of the Oscars ceremony.
Fox News Channel, whose opinion leaders have clearly supported Kavanaugh's appointment, was the network with the highest audience, with an average of 5.69 million viewers during the witness for an entire day, according to Nielsen.
ABC came second with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS grossed 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million, according to data from Nielsen.
Interest remains high after testimony.
Flake was the central figure of Friday's drama.
After the moderate Republican's office issued a statement saying they would vote for Kavanaugh, Flake was captured by CNN and CBS cameras on Friday morning while he was rebuked by protesters when he was trying to board an elevator to go to a Judicial Affairs Committee view.
Flake spent several minutes with his sight dejected as he was resumed live on television on CNN.
"Here I'm, in front of you," a woman said.
“Do you think you’re telling the truth to the country?”
They told him 'you have power, while there are so many women who don't have one!'
Flake said that his office had issued a statement and, before the elevator doors were closed, he commented that he will have more things to say in view of the committee.
After a few hours, all the cable and broadcast chains were already broadcasting live the moment when the Judicial Affairs Committee had to vote whether or not to go ahead with Kavanauugh's appointment in the plenary session of the Senate to submit it to a vote.
Flake said, instead, that he would only understand that the FBI would investigate allegations against the nominee ahead of next week, a condition demanded by the minority of Democrats.
Flake was partly persuaded thanks to the conversations he had with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After a subsequent conversation with Coons and several senators, Flake made his decision.
The option chosen by Flake had influence, as it was evident that Republicans wouldn't have enough votes to support Kavanaugh without the investigation.
President Trump has opened an FBI investigation over Kavanaugh's allegations.
Britain’s Prime Minister May accuses critics of “playing politics” in relation to Brexit
Prime Minister Theresa May accused those criticizing their plans to leave the European Union to “play politics” with the UK’s future and to disparage national interest in an interview with Sunday Times.
British Prime Minister Theresa May arrives at the Conservative party congress in Birmingham, United Kingdom, on September 29, 2018.
In another interview in addition to that conducted with May on the front page of the newspaper, the former foreign affairs minister of May Boris Johnson reinforced his attack on the so-called Chequers plan for Brexit, where he states that the proposal that the United Kingdom and the EU collect mutual taxes is “completely absurd”.
Wayde Sims shot death: Police detain suspect Dyteon Simpson for LSU team player's death
Police have arrested a suspect for the shooting murder of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player working in the LSU team.
Dyteon Simpson, 20, has been arrested and sent to prison charged with second-degree murdering, according to Baton Rouge's policing department.
Authorities made public a video of the confrontation between Sims and Simpson. According to police, Sims lost his glasses during the brawl.
Police retrieved their glasses at the crime scene and claims they have found Simpson's DNA on them, according to CBS affiliate WAFB.
After questioning Top Simpson, he admitted to killing Wayde from a gunshot, according to police.
His bail has been set at $350 $350 000, the lawyer reported.
The East Baton Rouge district forensic office on Friday released a preliminary report, stating that the cause of death was a firearmight wound to the head towards the neck.
The department thanks the operational group of fugitives from the state of Louisiana, in the state police criminalistics lab, Southern University policing and citizens in the area for the aid rendered in the investigation that has culminated in this arrest.
LSU Sports Director Joe Alleva expressed his gratitude to law enforcement in the area for his “diligence and for procuring justice.”
Sims was 20 years old.
Media 1.98 m, played as a forward and grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father, Wayne, was also a basketball player on the LSU team.
He averaged 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds to start last season.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade commented that the team is “destroyed” and that Wayde’s death was “a very strong blow.”
"Things like this are what make you suffer at all times," Wade said.
An ash spit volcano in Mexico City
Ash sprinkled by the Popocatepetl volcano has reached the southern neighborhoods of Mexico's capital.
The national disaster prevention center warned Mexicans on Saturday to stay away from the volcano after activity was detected in the crater and 183 emissions of gas and ash were recorded over a 24-hour period.
The center was overseeing several thunders and tremors that had occurred recently.
Images on social networks where you can see a thin layer of ash on the windshields of cars in neighborhoods of Mexico City such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have detected an increase in volcano activity located 72 km southeast of the capital since an earthquake of magnitude 7.1 shooked central Mexico in September 2017.
The volcano, known as "Don Goyo", has remained active since 1994.
The police clash with Catalan separatists shortly before the anniversary of the referendum on independence
Six people were arrested in Barcelona last Saturday after pro-independence protesters clashed with riot police and thousands of people joined contrary demonstrations to remember the first anniversary of the vote that took place in Catalonia about their secession.
A group of pro-independence individuals detained by anti-riot police began throwing them eggs and powder paint, which led to the appearance of dark clouds of dust on the streets normally invaded by tourists.
Later the same day, there were skirmishes with the police, who used their batons in order to contain the violent protesters.
For several hours, pro-independence groups sang “Let’s not forget, let’s not forgive” face-to-face with unionist protesters shouting “Longe life in Spain.”
Fourteen people had to be treated for minor injuries suffered during the protests, local sources said.
Tensions have not diminished in this pro-independence-minded region one year after a referendum considered illegal by Madrid but also carried out by Catalan separatists was held on 1 October.
Voters chose independence by a very large majority, although the turnout rate was low, given that opponents of secession mostly boycotted voting.
According to Catalan authorities, almost 1000 people were injured last year after the police tried to stop the vote by entering polling stations throughout the region, which caused violent clashes.
Pro-independence groups encamped all night on Friday to avoid a demonstration in support of national police.
The demonstration took place anyway, but protesters were forced to take another route.
Narcís Termes, a 68-year-old electrician and one of the participants in the pro-independence demonstration together with his wife, said he no longer had many hopes about the chances that Catalonia will achieve independence.
“Last year we had one of the best moments.
I saw my parents weeping with joy because they could vote, but now we're stranded," he said.
Despite achieving a vital victory, albeit by little margin, in the regional elections last December, the Catalan pro-independence parties have suffered to maintain the momentum this year, given that many of their main leaders are in self-imposed exile or detainees waiting to be tried for their role in the organization of the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic, records the demonstration of support for the police with his phone, and affirms that the conflict has been fueled by politicians on both sides.
"Tension does nothing but increase," he says.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of the nine Catalan leaders jailed before the trial since the end of last year, announced that he would participate in elections to the European Parliament next year.
“Presenting himself as a candidate in the European elections is the best way to denounce the decline in democratic values and the repression that we have seen by the Spanish government,” he says.
Londonderry: Men arrested after raising a house with a car
Three men aged 33, 34 and 39 have been arrested after a car rammed repeatedly against a house in Londonderry.
The incident occurred at Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday around 19:30 local time.
Inspector Bob Blemmings says damage was caused to the doors and the building itself.
It is also possible that, at some point, someone has shot a crossbow at the car.
A play by Menga gives Livingston's 1-0 win over Rangers
Dolly Menga's first goal with Livingston meant victory
Livingston, who this year had risen to category, surprised Glasgow Rangers in what is barely Steven Gerrard's second defeat in 18 games as coach of Ibrox neighborhood club.
Dolly Menga's move made a difference, since, with this victory, Gary Holt's team rise to the second position, which they share with Hibernian.
Gerrard, on the other hand, remain without winning away from home in the Premiership this season. Next Sunday they will face the leader, the Hearts, with whom they are separated by an eight-point difference.
But before that, the Rangers on Thursday received Rapid from Vienna in the Europa League.
Livingston, in the meantime, extends their undefeated streak in the league to six games, with a Holt as coach who still doesn't know what defeat is since replacing Kenny Miller last month.
The Livingston does not take advantage of its opportunities in front of the direct game of the visitors
Holt's team should have gone ahead on the scorer long before the goal, given that their direct game caused all sorts of problems for Rangers.
Scott Robinson made his way between the defense, but he squambled his chance when he found himself in front of the goal. Afterwards, Alan Lithgow sent the ball away when he stretched out to try to finish Craig Halkett's header pass, who crossed the goal.
It was good for the locals to let Rangers get closer to their area, as they knew they could put the visitors in trouble on isolated moves.
And that's how this decisive goal came about.
The referee whistled a foul against Rangers' interests and Livingston made an opening to the band, where Declan Gallagher and Robinson combined so that Menga could play it first and finish from the central part of the area.
Until then, Rangers had mastered possession, but encountered an impenetrable defence and goalkeeper Liam Kelly who had very little work throughout the game,
The stage was repeated in the second half, although Alfredo Morelos forced Kelly to make a stop of merit.
For his part, Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor stopped with his feet an occasion from Scott Pittman and Lithgow finished deflected another combination play from Livingston.
The Rangers dedicated himself to hanging again and again balls in the Livingston area, only to be rejected. In addition, two penalties were claimed, one for an alleged entry of Halkett to substitute Glenn Middleton, and another for hands. Both were dismissed.
“Phenomenal” Livingston: analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland at the Tony Macaroni Arena
Great performance and great result for Livingston.
A man thinks that they were excellent, and they continue to exceed all expectations in this upward trajectory.
His style of play and staff have hardly changed since his return to the top category, although much of the merit must be attributed to Holt because of the way he has galvanized the team since his arrival.
There was a good bunch of heroes.
Captain Halkett was immense, at the head of a perfectly organized defence, while Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall had to be aware of Menga throughout the match.
Rangers lacked inspiration, however.
Despite the brilliant moments exhibited with Gerrard as coach, they have definitely not been able to maintain the level.
They didn't know how to create chances (only once they clearly overcame the local defense), and that the Rangers, which is in the middle of the table, should take it as a warning.
Bittersweet reception for Erdogan in Cologne
It was all clear smiles and skies on Saturday (September 29) when the leaders of Turkey and Germany gathered for breakfast in Berlin.
It was the first day of President Erdogan's controversial visit to Germany, which aimed to repair relations between NATO's two allies.
These had deteriorated due to issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey's entry into the EU.
Erdogan then went to Cologne to inaugurate a new giant mosque.
And it is that this city has the largest Turkish population in the world outside of Turkey.
The police alleged security reasons to prevent a crowd of 25 000 people from congregating in front of the mosque, although a large number of followers gathered in the vicinity to be able to see its president.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters, many of them Kurds, made themselves heard while condemning both Erdogan's policies and the German government's decision to welcome him to the country.
Both manifestations reflect the division of opinions that exist around the visitor, considered a hero by some German Turks and branded a dictator by others.
Deptford Traffic Accident: Cyclist Dies in Hitting a Car
A cyclist has been killed as a result of a collision with a car in London.
The crash took place near the junction of Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street, an area with plenty of traffic in Deptford, southeast of the city, around 10:15 local time.
The driver of the vehicle stopped and, although the paramedical services attended to the cyclist, he died on the spot.
The accident comes months after another cyclist died in an escape accident case at Childers Street, 1.6 km from Saturday's crash site.
Metropolitan police have said their officers are working to identify the man and report the facts to the closest relatives.
The street has been cut and deviations have been applied for bus routes. In addition, drivers have been advised to avoid the area.
Long Lartin Prison: Six Officers Injured in Altercation
Six jailors have been injured in an altercation in this high-security men's prison, according to the center's office.
The events occurred at Long Lartin, Worcestershire, around 09:30 on Sunday, local time, and it still persists.
Specialized “tornado” agents moved to the place of the events to put an end to the altercation, in which eight inmates have been involved. The riot could be contained on the one hand.
Officers had to be treated for minor injuries to their faces at the scene.
“We have deployed specially trained prison personnel to try to solve an incident produced in Long Martin prison, which still persists.
Six employees had to be cared for with injuries.
We do not tolerate violence in our prisons. We are clear that those responsible for this altercation will have to be held accountable to the police and they could have to spend even more time in the garjola.”
More than 500 inmates are found at the Long Lartin Penitential Centre, including some of the most dangerous criminals.
In June, it became known that the head of the prison had to be treated in a hospital after being attacked by an inmate.
In addition, in October last year, riot police officers made an appearance in prison to solve a serious altercation in which personnel from the center were attacked with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa Threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City With Major Floats (Drought Areas Could Benefit)
It is not usual for a tropical depression to affect Arizona, but this is precisely what is likely to happen early next week when what remains of Hurricane Rosa passes through the Southwest desert, with the consequent risk of floods.
The national weather service has already issued flood alerts for Monday and Tuesday west of Arizona to the south and east of Nevada, south-east California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to arrive directly to Phoenix on Tuesday and to get close to it on Monday night with rains.
Phoenix's national meteorological service commented in a tweet that only "ten tropical cyclones have maintained the state of storm or tropical depression within a 322-km radius of Phoenix since 1950.
Hurricane Katrina (1967) stayed 64 km from the Arizona border.”
The most recent models of the national hurricane center forecast from 50 to 100 mm of rain, with isolated points where up to 150 mm would be recorded in the Mogollon of Arizona.
Other areas of the south-west desert, including the Central Rockies and the Great Basin, could register from 25 to 50 mm, with the possibility of isolated points up to 100 mm.
For areas that are at risk of floods, the Rosa rain could become very good news, given that the region suffers a severe drought.
Although floods are a serious cause for concern, some of the rainfall could be very beneficial, since the southwest region currently suffers a drought.
According to the U.S. drought control service, more than 40 % of Arizone state suffers at least extreme drought, the second-highest category, reports weather.com.
But first, Hurricane Rosa will make landfall on the Baja California Peninsula in Mexico.
El Rosa continued to have hurricane strength Sunday morning, with maximum winds of 137 km/h, and is now 620 km south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico, moving in a northerly direction at a speed of 20 km/h.
The storm is encountering coldest waters in the Pacifics, which causes it to lose strength.
It is therefore expected to make landfall in Mexico with tropical storm force in the afternoon or evening on Monday.
Raising in different parts of Mexico could be strong, with a considerable risk of flooding.
According to weather.com, “75 to 150 mm of rain is expected from Baja California to the northwest of Sonora, with the possibility that they will reach up to 250 mm”.
After that, the Rosa will head north across Mexico with tropical storm force, before reaching the border with Arizona in the early hours of Tuesday morning with tropical depression, to finally cross the state of Arizona and reach southern Utah well into Tuesday night.
“The main risk that we expect from the Rose or what remains of it are very heavy rainfall in Baja California, north-west of Sonora and the south-west U.S. desert, according to the national hurricane center.
Rains are expected to cause dangerous floods and waste flows into deserts, as well as landslides in mountainous areas.
Midsomer Norton attack: four arrested for attempted murder
Three teenagers and a 20-year-old man have been arrested on suspicion of attempted murder after a 16-year-old boy has been stabbed in Somerset.
The teenager was found injured in Midsomer Norton's Excelsior Terrace area, around 4:00 on Saturday, local time.
He was taken to the hospital, where he is "stable."
A 17-year-old boy, 18-year-old and 20-year-old man were arrested the following day in the Radstock area, according to Avon and Somerset police.
Officers have made an appeal so that anyone who can have some kind of recording of the facts with the mobile phone gets in touch.
Trump says Kavanaugh “has suffered the pettyness and anger” of the Democratic party
"The vote for Judge Kavanaugh is the vote of rejection of the ruthless and outrageous tactics of the Democratic party," Trump decreed at an e-election in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump said Kavanaugh "suffered the pettyness and anger" of the Democratic party throughout his appointment process.
Kavanaugh testified before Congress on Thursday, bluntly and emotionally denying Christine Blasey Ford's indictment of sexually abusing her decades ago, when both were teenagers.
Ford also testified in view in connection with his plea.
The president said on Saturday that “the American people saw Kavanaugh’s genius, human quality and courage.”
“The vote to ratify Judge Kavanaugh is a vote to ratify one of the brightest minds in the legal world of our time, a jurist with an admirable track record of service to the public.”
The president indirectly referred to Kavanaugh's appointment as he talked about the importance of Republican voter turnout rate in mid-term elections.
“We are five weeks away from one of the most important elections of our lives.
I'm not running, but I don't really go there," he said.
"That's why I'm going everywhere fighting for our great candidates."
Trump argued that Democrats are engaged in “resisting and clogging.”
The first key procedural vote to be held in the senate on Kavanaugh's appointment is expected to take place no later than Friday, an attendee of Republican party leaders told CNN.
Hundreds killed by earthquake and tsunami in Indosñona, where the death toll continues to grow
At least 384 people have died, and many have been swept away by the giant waves crashing into the beaches, due to a serious earthquake and tsunami that has affected the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, authorities said on Saturday.
Hundreds of people had gathered on Friday on the occasion of a festival held on the beach in the city of Palu when waves of up to six meters hit the coast towards sunset, dragging many of the people to death and destroying everything they found on their way.
The tsunami was followed by an earthquake of magnitude 7.5.
“When the tsunami threat arrived yesterday, people were still carrying out their activities on the beach and didn’t run right away. That’s why they became victims,” said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the BNPB disaster mitigation agency in a statement in Jakarta.
"The tsunami didn't come alonely, it dragged cars, logs, houses, destroyed everything on the ground," Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami had shifted by open sea at speeds of 800 km/h before hitting the coast.
There are people who climbed up a tree fleeing the tsunami and were able to survive, he says.
Some 16 700 people had to be evacuated to 24 different centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the disaster mitigation agency where you can appreciate the number of shops and destroyed buildings, turned and collapsed bridges and a mosque surrounded by water.
The aftershocks continued to shake this coastal city on Saturday.
Earthquake series were felt in an area of 2.4 million inhabitants.
The Indonesian technology evaluation and enforcement agency (BPPT) said in a statement that the energy released by Friday's intense earthquake was about 200 times the power of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima in World War II.
According to the agency, the geographical characteristics of the city, located at the end of a long and narrow bay, could have exponentially increased the magnitude of the tsunami.
Nugroho described the damage as “considerable” and said that thousands of homes, hospitals, malls and hotels collapsed.
The bodies of some of the victims, he comments, were found trapped among the rubble of the collapsed buildings, adding that 540 people were injured and 29 lost.
According to Nugroho, deaths and damage could rise on the coast about 300 km north of Palu, an area called Donggala, closer to the epicenter of the earthquake.
The communications “were totally paralyzed and therefore no information arrived” from Donggala, says Nugroho.
More than 300 000 people live there,” the Red Cross commented in a statement, adding that its workers and volunteers were heading towards the affected areas.
"This is already a tragedy, which unfortunately could end much worse," he says.
The agency was heavily criticised on Saturday for failing to report the arrival of a tsunami in Palu, although officers said the waves arrived during the time the warning was issued.
In a recording of an amateur shared on social media, you can hear how a man located on the top floor of a building gives desperate shout-out of warning to ordinary people about the approaching tsunami.
In a matter of minutes, a wall of water explodes on the seashore, taking buildings and vehicles.
Reuters couldn't immediately authenticate the recording.
The earthquake and tsunami have caused a widespread cut of the electricity supply that has disrupted communications around Palu, making it difficult for the authorities to coordinate the rescue tasks.
Although the army has begun to send humanitarian aid on cargo aircraft from Jakarta and other cities, according to authorities, evacuees are still desperately needing food, in addition to other basic necessities.
The city's airport has been reopened only for relief tasks and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo planned to visit the evacuation centers in Palu on Sunday.
The death toll from the Indonesian tsunami is above 800.
The situation is very serious.
Although World Vision workers in Donggala were able to arrive safely in the city of Palu, where employees are sheltered in canvas shelters installed in the courtyard of their office, during the journey they had to witness scenes of total destruction, says Doseba.
"They told me they saw a lot of destroyed houses," he said.
It's a catastrophe.
Although aid groups made the bleak gesture of initiating relief work, some people complained that humanitarian workers were being prevented from traveling to Palu.
In accordance with Indonesian regulations, the collection of funds, provisions and the endowment of personnel from abroad can only begin to flow if the place where a catastrophe has occurred is declared a national disaster zone.
That hasn't happened yet.
"It's still a disaster at the provincial level," said Aulia Arriani, spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
"Once the government says "well, it's a national disaster," we can start asking for international assistance, but this level hasn't yet been declared."
While falling the second night in Palu after the earthquake and tsunami that took place on Friday, the friends and relatives of the missing people maintained the hope that the miracle would take place and their loved ones would elude the bleak account of natural disasters.
On Saturday, a kild was rescued trapped in a sewer.
On Sunday, rescue crews freed a woman who had been buried under the rubble for two days with her mother's body next door.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of the Indonesian paragliding national team, had coached two of the missing paragliders for the Asian Games, which concluded earlier this month in Indonesia.
In addition to Mr. Mandagi, among the people trapped in the Roa Roa hotel, were his students.
"As a senior in paragliding, I have my own emotional burden," he said.
Mr. Gendon explained that, in the hours after the news circulates of the collapse of the Roa Roa hotel among the paragliding community, he had desperately sent messages on WhatsApp to Palu competitors who were participating in the beach festival.
However, the messages appeared with a gray checkmark instead of a blue double mark.
"I think this means that the messages were not received," he added.
Thieves take $26,750 while filling an ATM at Newport on the Levee
Last Friday morning, thieves stole $26,750 from a Brink's worker who was filling an ATM at Newport on the Levee, according to a press release issued by the Newport police department.
Agent Dennis McCarthy indicated in the statement that the driver of the vehicle was emptying the ATM at the entertainment complex and was preparing it to distribute more money.
While doing his job, another man "came running behind Brink's employee" and stole a bag of money destined for distribution.
According to the statement, witnesses spotted several suspects fleeing the scene, but police did not specify the number of those involved in the incident.
Anyone who has information about their identities should contact Newport police at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: the rapper is renamed Ye
Rapper Kanye West is renamed Ye.
On Saturday, while announcing the change on Twitter, he wrote: "Being formally known as Kanye West."
It's been some time in West's 41-year-old, it's been the nickname Ye, and used this alias as the title of their eighth album, which was released in June.
The change occurs prior to his appearance on the Saturday Night Live show, in which he is expected to present his new album, titled Yandhi.
The rapper replaces singer Ariana Grande, who canceled her participation for "emotional reasons," according to the show's author.
Apart from being an abbreviation of his current stage name, West had previously commented that the word has a religious meaning for him.
"I think ye is the most widely used word in the Bible, and in the Bible means you," West said earlier this year, while discussing the title of his album with radio presenter Big Boy.
"So it's you, it's us.
I've gone from Kanye, which means the only one, to simply Ye, which is a reflection of our good side, our bad side, our confusing part, everything.
"The album is rather a reflection of who we are."
West is part of a series of famous rappers who have changed their name.
Sean Combs has been known by various names, such as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year he has announced his preference for the names Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, West's former collaborator, has used his nickname with or without a hyphen and in capital letters.
Mexico's president, AMLO, vows not to use military force against civilians
The president-elect of Mexico, Andrés Manuel Lopez Obrador, has promised never to use military force against civilians, when little is left for the country to celebrate the 50th anniversary of bloody reprisal against students.
López Obrador promised on Saturday in Tlatelolco Square that "military force would never again be used to repress the Mexican people."
On October 2, 1968, the army opened fire during a peaceful demonstration and resulted in the deaths of 300 people at a time when left-wing student student movements were taking root throughout Latin America.
López Obrador, who is committed to supporting young Mexicans by granting monthly grants to students and opening up more free public universities,
He has stated that unemployment and a lack of educational opportunities make young people attracted to criminal gangs.
