"While China is becoming increasingly active in the field of artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double the amount of money spent on research in this field," said Kai-Fu Lee, an investor and professional specializing in artificial intelligence who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments come after several members of the U.S. government have announced AI measures, despite the fact that in the country there is a general lack of formal strategy in AI.
Meanwhile, China unveiled its plan last year: its goal is to become the world's No. 1 in terms of innovation in AI by 2030.
"Doubling the budget allocated to AI research would be a good start, since the rest of the countries are far behind the U.S. and we want to be the pioneers of the next advances in the field of AI," Lee said.
"Doubling funding could double opportunities for the U.S. to achieve the next major discovery in AI," Lee told CNBC in an interview conducted this week.
Lee, author of the book titled AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order, published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of the Sinovation Ventures society, which has invested in Face++, one of China's most prominent AI companies.
In 1980 he worked at Carnegie Mellon University in an AI system that surpassed Othello's American player with the best classification and subsequently served as an executive at Microsoft Research and as president of Google's Chinese branch.
Lee positively valued previous technology competitions from the U.S. Government, such as the Robotics Challenge of the Advanced Defense Research Project Agency (DARPA), and wondered when the next one would take place in order to identify the next visionaries.
"U.S. researchers often have to work hard to obtain public scholarships," Lee said.
"It's not China that takes the academic leaders; it's companies."
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have hired university eminences to work in the field of AI.
Lee commented that U.S. work in the field of AI could also be boosted with changes in immigration policy.
"I think that permanent residence cards should be automatically offered to doctoral students in AI," he added.
In July 2017, China's State Council presented its state-of-the-art Artificial Intelligence Development Plan.
"China's National Natural Science Foundation grants funds to people from academic institutions just as the Natural Sciences Foundation and other U.S. public bodies give money to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work is lower in China," Lee said.
At the beginning of the year, the U.S. Department of Defense created a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, designed to involve collaborators in the industrial sector and academic institutions, and the White House announced the constitution of a Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
In addition, this month the DARPA has announced an investment of 2,000 million USD in an initiative called AI Next.
Regarding the Natural Sciences Foundation, it currently allocates more than 100 million USD per year to AI research.
Meanwhile, no measures have been applied in relation to U.S
The Macedonians hold a referendum to decide if they change the name of the country
Last Sunday, the Macedonian people held a referendum on the change of name to "Republic of Northern Macedonia", a measure that would resolve a dispute that has existed for decades with Greece, which had blocked applications for accession of Macedonia to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, maintains that the name of its northern neighbor constitutes a claim of its territory and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments reached an agreement on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic-majority population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has stated that he will not participate in the referendum, and a boycott campaign will doubt whether it will reach the minimum necessary turnout of 50% for the referendum to be valid.
“Are you in favor of accession to NATO and the EU, with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?”.
Supporters of name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that it's a worth-paying price to achieve accession from Macedonia, one of the countries that were created in the wake of Yugoslavia's disintegration, to bodies such as the EU and NATON.
"Today I came to vote for the future of the country, so that the young people of Macedonia can live freely under the protection of the European Union, because it means that all of us will have a safer life," Olivera Georgijevska, 79, in Skopje.
Although it is not legally binding, a sufficient number of parliamentarians have stated that they will abide by the result of the vote to make it decisive.
To change the name, a two-thirds majority is required in Parliament.
The State Electoral Commission reported that there had been no irregularity at 1 p.m.
However, turnout was only 16%, compared to 34% registered in the last parliamentary elections, held in 2016, in which 66% of those registered voted.
"I've come to vote for my children, our place is in Europe," Gjose Tanevski, 62, told Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dushko voted in the referendum held in Medacon on the country's name change, which would open the route of accession to the European Union and NATO to Strumica, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In front of Skopje's Parliament, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, was preparing a small stage and placing chairs in front of the tents installed by the people who would boycott the referendum.
"We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to stick to it with our heads held high, not through the back door," Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don't want to accept us as Macedonia, we can resort to other countries, such as China and Russia, and become part of Eurasian integration."
Prime Minister Zaev states that incorporation into NATO will be accompanied by more than necessary investments in Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20%.
"I believe that the vast majority will vote in favor, because more than 80% of citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said before depositing his ballot.
Zaev stated that a "yes" would mean the "confirmation of our future".
In a survey published last Monday by the Political Research Institute of Macedonia, between 30% and 43% of voters would participate in the referendum, a figure below the minimum turnout required.
According to the results of another survey, led by the Macedonian channel Telma TV, 57% of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70% stated that they would vote "yes".
For the referendum to be valid, the turnout must be 50% plus a vote.
A failure in the referendum would be the first severe blow to pro-Western Government policy since it took over in May last year.
Watch the video: Sergio Agüero, from Manchester City, makes a goal after dribbling all of Brighton's defence
Sergio Agüero and Raheem Sterling nullified Brighton's defence in Manchester City's 2-0 victory in the match played at Etihad Stadium, Manchester (England) last Saturday.
Agüero scored a goal in the 65th minute in a play that seemed ridiculously simple.
The Argentinian striker received a pass in midfield at the start of the sequence.
He marched by speed between three Brighton defenders, ravaging with what came out of him on the pitch.
Afterwards, Agüero was surrounded by four green T-shirts.
He dribbled a defender before overcominging a few more on board the Brighton area.
He then made a pass to his left who found Sterling.
The English striker returned the ball to the first touch, inside the area, to Agüero, who with his right leg beat Brighton's goalkeeper, Mathew Ryan, with a shot that sneaked inside the net on the right side.
"Agüero has discomfort in the foot," Pep Guardiola, City's coach, told reporters.
"We've decided I'd play between 55 and 60 minutes.
That's what happened.
We're lucky he's scored a goal at the time."
But it was Sterling who gave the initial advantage to the Sky Blues in the fight for the Premier League.
His goal came in the 29th minute.
In that play, Agüero received the ball in depth from the Brighton and
made a beautiful passage into space on the left side towards Leroy Sane.
Sane drove the ball before passing it to Sterling at the long post.
Sky Blues striker topped the ball inside the net before going out along the bottom line.
