The Rockies secured a wildcard with a 5–2 win against the Nationals on Friday night, but are still looking to secure their first division title.
"Although we don't play anything, at least we can go out and throw knowing the atmosphere here, in Denver, and knowing that the other team will probably play each point at the highest level, as no one has done this year.
“Why would I want to miss this game?”
The Nationals have yet to announce an opener launcher for Sunday, but are apparently determined to let Scherzer launch if this situation occurs.
Scherzer, who would make his 34th opening, did a warm-up session on Thursday and would launch Sunday, on his usual resting day.
Washington's right-handed pitcher has an 18-7 record, with an ERA of 2.53 and 300 eliminations for strikes at 220 2/3 entries this season.
Trump holds rally in West Virginia
The president indirectly referred to the situation around his candidate for the Supreme Court, Brett Kavanaugh, while discussing the importance of Republican turnout in mid-term elections.
"Everything we've done is at stake in November.
There are five weeks left for one of the most important elections of our lives.
This is one of the big ones.. I don't show up, but in reality I'm introduced. That's why I'm everywhere fighting for the fantastic candidates," he said.
Trump continued: 'You can see this horrific, horrible group of Democrats, it's happening right now.
They are determined to regain power by all necessary means, you see their pettyness, their bad intention.
They don’t care about those who hurt, above who they have to go through to gain power and control. That’s what they want: power and control. But we won’t give them.”
Democrats' mission, he said, is "resisting and clogging."
"And this can be seen in the last four days," he riveted, calling Democrats "angry, petty, dirty and false."
Trump mentioned Dianne Feinstein, the highest-ranking democratic senator on the Senate Judiciary Committee, which provoked a loud shout-out from the public.
“Do you remember your answer?
Have you leaked the document?
Hey, hey, what?
No, huh, no. Await a moment... This was a very bad body language expression, the worst body language I've ever seen."
The Labour Party has ceased to be a diverse party.
It is intolerant with those who say what they think.
When Momentum activists from my local party voted to censor me, I wasn't shocked.
After all, I'm the last of a series of British parliamentarians to whom we've been told we're not welcome, all to say what we think.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan received similar treatment because she manifested with determination against anti-Semitism.
In my case, with the no-confidence motion I was criticized for disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe. Ironically, they are similar issues with respect to which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
In the notice of Friday's Nottingham Labour Party meeting, it was communicated that "we want meetings to be inclusive and productive."
For most of my eight years as a local Labour parliamentarian, the meetings of the General Committee held on Friday night have been exactly like this.
Unfortunately, this is not the tone of many meetings, and for a long time the promise of "more generous and friendly" policies has been forgotten, if it ever existed.
It is increasingly clear that divergent opinions are not tolerated in the Labour Party, and each opinion is judged based on whether it is acceptable to party leadership.
This began shortly after Jeremy became the leader, since colleagues with whom he previously thought he shared a similar political vision expected me to take a 180o turn and take positions he would never have adopted, either on national security or on the EU single market.
As long as I speak in public, whatever it says, social media is filled with aggressive messages in which a call is made so that my candidacy is not renewed, center policies are denounced and I am told that it should not be in the Labour Party.
And that doesn't just happen to me.
In fact, I know that I have better luck than some of my colleagues, since the messages I receive are usually political.
I am impressed by the professionalism and determination of colleagues who every day have to deal with a torrent of sexist and racist attacks, and don't shrug.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political era is to see how the level of abuse has normalised.
Jeremy Corbyn last week stated that the Labor Party should foster a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer that game so diverse, and with every motion of "unconfidence" or every change in the rules of selection, the match is tightening more.
In the last two years I have received many tips and I have been advised to keep my head down, that it was not so expressive and "everything would be fine".
But I didn't get into politics for it.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago, in my time as a student, stimulated by Thatcher's negligent government, which left my entire class literally sunk, I have tried to defend better public services for those who need it most, either as a local councillor or as a government minister.
I've never concealed my politics, not even in the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could doubt in any way about my political positions and points of disagreement with the current leadership.
To those who promoted the motion on Friday, I would say that at a time when the country is moving towards a Brexit that will harm households, businesses and our public services, I don't understand the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the leader of the Labour Party.
But, in reality, the message I want to convey is not directed towards Nottingham Momentum, but towards my constituents, whether they are Labour members or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise you that no threat of non-renewal of my candidacy or no political interest will prevent me from acting, in my opinion, for the benefit of the interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a Member of Parliament at Nottingham East
Ayr 38 - 17 Melrose: some unbeaten Ayr lead
Two last-minute trials distorted the final result, but there's no doubt that the Ayrs deserved victory in this vibrant Tennent's Premiership Day match.
The Ayrs, who are the only unbeaten team of the ten, occupy the first position in the leaderboard.
In the end, the local team prevailed thanks to their defensive superiority and the fact that they knew how to take better advantage of the opportunities, and coach Peter Murchie had reason to be satisfied.
"The games we've played so far have put us to the test and we're still undefeated, so I have to be happy," he said.
