«The sea is ours»: Bolivia, without a coastline, expects the court to reopen the route to the Pacific
Sailors patrulate by the naval headquarters covered in La Paz.
The public buildings wave a navy blue flag.
The naval bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon are filled with the motto: "The sea is ours by right.
Recovering it is a duty.”
Throughout Bolivia's coastless territory, the memory of a lost coast in front of Chile in a bloody resource conflict in the 19th century remains very vivid, as is the yearning to re-navigate the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes are perhaps the deepest in decades, while Bolivia awaits the resolution that the International Court of Justice will issue on October 1 after five years of deliberations.
"Bolivia has the impulse, a spirit of unity and serenity, and, needless to say, she expects the result with a positive stance," said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will look at the ICJ resolution on big screens across the country, in the hope that the Hague Court will dictate in favor of Bolivia's claim that, after decades of intermittent talks, Chile is obliged to negotiate the granting of a sovereign exit to the sea for Bolivia.
Evo Moralas, the charismatic indigenous president of Bolivia, who faces a controversial battle for re-election next year, also has many hopes deposited in Monday’s resolution.
"We're very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean," he vowed last August.
However, some analysts think that it is unlikely that the Court will decide in favor of Bolivia and that, if it did, things would not change much.
The Netherlands-based U.N U.S. body has no power to adjudicate Chilean territory and has established that it will not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The ICJ resolution, which comes just six months after the final allegations were presented, indicates that the case "was not complicated," said Paz Zárate, a Chilean specialist in international law.
And far from being favored, Bolivia's cause may have experienced a setback over the past four years.
"The current Bolivian administration has hijacked the issue of access to the sea," Zárate said.
"Morales' belligerent rhetoric has undermined any gesture of goodwill on the part of Chile," he suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to talk at some point, but it will be very difficult to have conversations after this episode.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
The previous president Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, representative of Bolivia in The Hague, rejected the idea that the decision-making of the Court was exceptionally quick.
Next Monday Bolivia will have "an extraordinary opportunity to start a new era of relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "put an end to 139 years of discrepancies, with mutual benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who remains one of the most popular presidents in Latin America, was using the maritime issue as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never give up on its right to access the Pacific Ocean," she added.
"Resolution is an opportunity to realize that we have to overcome the past."
North Korea claims nuclear disarmament will not occur unless it can trust the U.S
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho asserts that his nation will not eliminate nuclear weapons if he cannot trust Washington.
Ri spoke last Saturday at the United Nations General Assembly.
The minister urged the U.S. to keep the promises made during a summit held in Singapore between the leaders of the two rival countries.
His remarks come at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo is about to resume stagnant nuclear diplomacy more than three months after the summit held in Singapore with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un.
Ri says it's a chimera to think that continued sanctions and U.S. objection to a statement ending the Korean War could cause North Korea to ever kneel.
Washington is wary of accepting the statement if Pyongyang doesn't make major disarmament movements first.
Both Kim and President Donald Trump want to hold a second summit.
However, there is widespread skepticism about Pyongyang taking seriously the question of giving up an arsenal that the country considers the only means to ensure its safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit between Kim and Trump.
Paris fashion shows reveal the latest range of oversized hats, on the way to a main street near you
If you want to increase the size of your collection of hats or want to protect yourself completely from the sun, you don't have to look any further.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne presented on the catwalk a range of extravagant hats of disproportionate dimensions from their SS19 collection, which dazzled the style defined at Paris Fashion Week.
This summer Instagram has been devastated by very impractical hats, and these designers have brought their amazing creations to the catwalk.
Valentino's outstanding piece was an exaggerated beige hat, adorned with a broad, feather-shaped wing, which flooded the models' head.
Among other oversized accessories, there were jewed watermelons, a sorcerer's hat and even a pineapple, but these are not designed to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also presented a selection of bizarre masks, just in time for Halloween.
Many brightly colored masks had sewn lips and looked more like Hannibal Lecter than haute couture.
One creation resembled a complete scuba diving equipment, with tube and glasses, while another looked like a melted ice cream cone.
And if you follow the big fashion trends, you're in luck.
Style observers predict that huge hats could be implanted on the main streets close to you.
Oversized hats trample the heels of "La Bomba", a straw hat, with a two-foot wide wing, which everyone has worn, from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult label behind the hat so impractical that it spread across social media brought another great creation to the catwalk: a straw bag for the beach, almost as large as the model dressed in swimsuit that was wearing it.
The raffia bag, dark orange, adorned with raffia fringes and finished with a white leather handle, was the outstanding piece of Jacquemus' La Riviera SS19 collection at Paris Fashion Week.
Famous stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: "I hope to see people wearing large hats and beach bags on the main streets next summer. Considering that the designer has had a great impact, it would be a shame that the demand for oversized accessories was ignored.
John Edward: Language skills are essential for world citizens
Independent schools in Scotland maintain a track record of academic excellence that has continued in 2018 with another series of results of excellent exams, which is reinforced with individual and collective success in sports, art, music and other community efforts.
With more than 30,000 students across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council for Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the best-level service to pupils and parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare students for later and higher studies, for the career they choose and for their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that can design and implement a personalized school curriculum, we observe that modern languages continue to be a very popular and desired subject of choice in schools.
"If you speak to a man in a language he understands, you're going to his head.
If you speak to him in his own language, you will come to his heart.”
This is a very important reminder that we cannot depend only on English if we want to establish relationships and generate trust with people from other countries.
Based on the recent results of this year's exams, we can see that the languages lead the classification tables with the highest qualifications in independent schools.
68% of the students who studied foreign languages obtained grade A higher.
The data, collected from 74 SCIS member schools reflected that 72% of students earned a higher A rating in Mandarin, while 72% of German students, 69% of French students and 63% of Spanish students also obtained an A.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as vital skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Currently, languages, as a subject of choice, are maintained in the same consideration as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in curricula from independent schools and elsewhere.
A survey conducted in 2014 by the UK Employment and Competences Commission revealed that of the reasons that employers gave to the fact that they found it difficult to cover vacancies, 17% was attributed to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, language skills are increasingly indispensable to prepare young people for their future careers.
Since there are more and more potential job opportunities that require languages, these competences are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the career that a person chooses, if they have learned a second language, they will have a real advantage in the future with a permanent competence like this.
Being able to communicate directly with people from other countries automatically puts a multilingual person ahead of the competition.
According to a survey carried out in 2013 by YouGov among more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom, 75% did not know how to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and French, with 15% of speakers, was the only language with a double-digit percentage.
For this reason, investing in language teaching is important for today's children.
Knowing several languages, especially those of developed economies, will give children better opportunities to find an important job.
In Scotland there is diversity among schools in terms of languages being taught.
Some schools focus more on more traditional modern languages, while others teach languages that are considered to be more important to the UK for the future, around 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child's interest, you can always choose from a series of languages in independent schools, which have teaching staff specialized in this field.
Independent Scottish schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children and endow them with the necessary skills to succeed, regardless of what the future holds.
In today's global commercial environment, it cannot be denied that languages continue to have a vital importance for the future of the country, and this must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should really be considered "international communicative competences."
Independent schools will continue to offer this option, diversity and excellence to young Scots.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
