Trump Meeting in West Virginia, YouTube Channel
President Donald Trump begins a wave of campaign events tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It is Trump's first five scheduled rallies for next week, which include visits to sympathetic sites, such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
While the confirmation vote for its candidate to fill the Supreme Court vacancy is awaiting, Trump intends to gain support for the next mid-term election, as Republicans are at risk of losing control of Congress when votes are cast in November.
What time is Trump's meeting in West Virginia tonight and how can it be seen online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 7 p.m. today, Saturday, September 29, 2018.
Trump's rally in West Virginia can be viewed online at the link specified below, via live video streaming on YouTube.
Trump is likely approaching the hearing started this week with Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh, who was strained due to allegations of sexual abuse she faces, while the Senate's planned confirmation vote remains on hold for up to a week and the FBI is investigating the case.
But the main objective of this wave of rallies is to help Republicans who stand in November's delicate election to gain momentum.
“Stimulating volunteers and sympathizers are aimed at ‘stimulating volunteers and sympathizers—while Republicans are trying to protect and expand the majorities they have in the Senate and the House of Representatives.
"Congress control is so important for his agenda that the president will travel to as many states as he can, while heading towards a busy campaign season," a Trump spokesperson said.
Tonight's meeting, scheduled at the Wesbanco Arena, Wheeling, could bring together "Ohio and Pennsylvania sympathizers, and attract Pittsburgh media coverage," according to West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday will be the 2nd time Trump has visited West Virginia, the state where he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help Patrick Morrisey, a Republican candidate for the Western Virginia Senate, who is getting unfavorable results in polls.
"It's not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come and give it momentum in the polls," commented Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, according to Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: The American team shows its ambition to keep hopes alive for Sunday's individual matches
After three uneven sessions, Saturday afternoon's foursomes were probably what this Ryder Cup needed.
The tilting pendulum of dynamics is an invented sports concept, in which players really believe, especially in competitions like this.
So on which side is the positive dynamic now?
"They had a six-point lead and now it's four, so I think we're in a small positive streak," Jordan Spieth said.
Obviously, Europe has an advantage, four points ahead, with twelve more at stake.
The Americans, as Spieth says, feel that the wind is blowing a bit in their favor and have many reasons to be encouraged, largely because of Spieth and Justin Thomas's state-of-the-run state, who played together all day and both have three-point points.
Spieth has been lethal since tee on the green and is setting an example.
Guttural celebration screams went up in intensity as he continued his vault and got a crucial putt to tie the fourth game, when he and Thomas had been twice down after two holes.
He celebrated the putt that made them win the game at hole 15 with a similar cry, of the kind indicating that he believes that the American team is not yet out of the competition.
"You have to make the most off and worry about your own party," Spieth said.
That's all these players have left now.
18 holes to leave footprint.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas the last two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the undisputed sensation of the Ryder Cup.
The strange but adorable European couple carry four out of four and don't make mistakes.
"Moliwood" was the only couple who didn't make a bogey Saturday afternoon, and also avoided bogeys Saturday morning and Friday afternoon, and last nine holes Friday morning.
Due to this result and the way his energy seems to flow to and from the bustling public, he reaffirms that they are the players to beat on Sunday, and there is not a more popular player than Fleetwood or Molinari to close a possible European victory while the sun sets over Le Golf National.
Preferably both at the same time in different holes.
However, talking about European glory seemed premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson quickly endeded with Sergio Garcia, the hero of the morning's fourballs, who partnered Alex Noren.
A bogey and two doubles in the first nine holes sank the Spaniard and Swede in a hole from which they never had any option to leave.
On Sunday, however, there is no one who can help you get out of the hole.
Fourballs and foursomes are very fascinating to see by the interactions between pairings, the tips they give and those they don't give, and the way a strategy can suddenly change.
Until now, Europe has played better as a team and will face the last day with an important advantage, but this foursome session has shown that the U.S. team has the motivation to fight that some, especially in the United States, had questioned.
Europe reaches Ryder Cup final with a favorable score of 10-6
Europe will face Ryder Cup last day with a comfortable lead after finishing Saturday's fourballs and foursomes with a 10-6 win over the U.S.
The inspired duo formed by Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari made a great effort to achieve two victories against a Tiger Woods in difficulties and leave their record, so far, by four points at Le Golf National.
The European team, captained by Thomas Bjorn, who aspired to retain the trophy he lost two years ago at Hazeltine, dominated an unwreathful American team in the morning's fourballs and put the series in 3-1.
The U.S. presented more resistance to the foursomes and won two games, but they couldn't reduce the differences.
Jim Furyk's team needed to get eight points in Sunday's 12 individual games to retain the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European debutant to achieve four consecutive points, while he and Molinari, acquainted with the nickname "Molliwood" after a sensational weekend, are only the second pair to win four points in their first four games in Ryder Cup history.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed in the fourballs, they curled a brilliant performance to beat a deflated Woods and American debutant Bryson Dechambeau for an even more emphatic 5&4.
Woods, who had dragged into two games Saturday, showed sporadic genius sparks, but has lost 19 out of 29 matches in fourballs and foursomes, and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, who did not participate in the fourballs in the morning, returned to pair with Henrik Stenson in the foursomes and fell 2-1 against Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, classified as first and third in the world, respectively.
However, Europe didn't have it all in its face on a pleasant and windy day southwest of Paris.
Jordan Spieth, three-time champion of the tournament, and Justin Thomas set a benchmark on Saturday for the Americans getting two points.
They achieved a 2–1 victory against Spaniard Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter in the fourballs and later again Poulter and Rory McIlroy 4–3 win at the foursomes, having lost the first two holes.
Only twice in Ryder Cup history one team had come back a four-point difference in individual matches, even though Furyk's team, defending the title, only needs to draw to preserve it.
However, after being second best for two days, it appears that a counter-attack next Sunday is beyond his reach.
North Korea declares that it will not unilaterally disarming "in any way" if there are no signs of confidence
The North Korean Minister of Foreign Affairs on Saturday informed the United Nations that the continuous sanctions were intensifying their distrust in the United States and that, in such circumstances, the country would not unilaterally eliminate, in any way, its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho told the annual General Assembly that North Korea had taken "important goodwill measures" over the previous year, such as stopping nuclear and missile tests, dismantling the nuclear test center and committing not to proliferate nuclear weapons and technology.
"However, we don't see any response accordingly from the U.S.," he said.
“Without trust in the United States, there will be no trust in our national security and, in these circumstances, we will not initiate disarmament unilaterally in any way.”
While Ri resumed North Korea's well-known complaints about Washington's resistance to an approach to denuclearization "in phases" by which North Korea would be rewarded as it took action gradually, his statements seemed significant because he did not reject full unilateral denuclearization, as Pyongyang had already done in the past.
Ri referred to the joint statement made by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit between an acting U.S. president and a North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, in which Kim pledged to work on "denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula," while Trump promised guarantees for North Korea's security.
North Korea has formally tried to end the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. has announced that Pyongyang must first eliminate its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted the call to relax the harsh international sanctions imposed on North Korea.
"The U.S. insists that denuclearization must first take place and increase the level of pressure with sanctions to achieve its goal coercively, in addition to opposing the "end-of-war declaration," Ri said.
“The perception that sanctions will make us kneel is a chimera of people who don’t know anything about us.
But the problem is that continuous sanctions are intensifying our distrust.”
Ri did not refer to the plans to hold a second summit between Kim and Trump that the U.S. leader highlighted to the United Nations earlier in the week.
Instead, the minister highlighted three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in in the past five months and added: "If childbirth involved in the denuclearization issue were South Korea instead of the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula would not have reached this stagnation situation."
However, the tone of Ri's speech was very different from what he held last year, when he declared at the U.N. General Assembly that targeting U.S. U.S. territory with North Korean missiles was inevitable after President "Mr. Evil" Trump called Kim "Rocket Man" a suicide mission.
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year threatened to "completely destroy" North Korea, praised Kim for his courage to take disarmament measures, but stated that there is still much to be done and sanctions must be maintained until North Korea becomes denuclearized.
Last Wednesday, Trump said there was no deadline for this and that "it doesn't matter if it takes two years, three years or five months."
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
"U.S. Secretary-of-State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that: "The implementation of Security Council sanctions must continue vigorously and without exception until we confirm total and final denuclearization."
The Security Council has unanimously prompted sanctions on North Korea since 2006 in a bid to cut funding for Pyongyang's ballistic and nuclear missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri outside the U.N. General Assembly and then announced that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn't go very well.
Pyongyang left in July saying that progress had been made, but after a few hours North Korea accused him of "demands typical of a gangster".
At a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle a missile center and a nuclear complex if the U.S. took "measures accordingly."
He said Kim had informed him that the "measures in line" he was looking for were the security measures that Trump had promised in Singapore, as well as actions towards the normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students take a course on resting habits
This year, a new course at Harvard University has allowed its students to sleep more, in an attempt to combat the increasingly widespread culture of studying throughout the night, stimulated by caffeine.
An academian observed that often students at number one university in the world are clueless about what goes to the basics on how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of Sleep Medicine at Harvard Medical School and specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed the course, which he thinks is the first of its kind in the United States.
It was inspired to create the course after giving a talk about the impact that sleep deprivation had on learning.
“At the end of the talk, a girl approached me and said, ‘Why do they tell me all this in my last year?”
"He didn't say anyone had ever told him about the importance of sleep, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
In the course, implemented for the first time this year, students are explained the basics on how good sleep habits improve academic and sports performance, in addition to general well-being.
Paul Barreira, Professor of Psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and Executive Director of the University's health services, stated that the University decided to enter the course after learning that some students were suffering severe sleep deprivation over the week.
The one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section there is a picture of a bedroom, where students have to click on coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books to receive information about the effects of caffeine and light, about how sports performance affects lack of sleep, and about the importance of maintaining a sleeping routine.
In another section, participants are informed about how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression, and cancer.
Next, a campus map with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily route.
"We know that the behavior of the students will not change immediately.
But we believe that they have the right to know it, as one has the right to know the effects it has for health to smoke cigarettes," Professor Czeisler said.
The pride culture of staying awake all night still exists, he said, and added that modern technology and the growing pressure suffered by students make sleep deprivation an ever-increasing problem.
Ensuring to enjoy good-quality sleep for long enough should be a "secret weapon" of students to combat stress, tiredness and anxiety, he added, even to avoid gaining weight, as sleep deprivation puts the brain in starvation mode and makes students always hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Californian who studies Chemistry and Physical Biology, helped Professor Czeisler design the course, having attended one of his classes last year during his first year at Harvard.
Raymond commented that the course had opened his eyes and inspired him to promote the course throughout the campus.
The next step, as he expects, is to ask postgraduate students to carry out a similar study program before joining the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommended that students take into account the fact of setting an alarm when they go to sleep and when they wake up, and to be aware of the detrimental effects of "blue light" emitted by electronic screens and LED lights, which can alter the harmony of circadian rhythm and cause sleep problems.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's goal ends Gerrard's men
Dolly Menga's goal condemns Steven Gerrard's disjointed team to a 1-0 home defeat from Livingston that represents another stumble outside.
The Ibrox team was looking to add their first victory at home since last February's 4–1 win in St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team inflicted the second defeat on Gerrard in 18 games as a coach to leave their team adrift, 8 points behind the Hearts, unstoppable leaders of the Ladbrokes Premiership.
Menga scored the goal seven minutes before half-time, and it never seemed that Rangers, lacking inspiration, were about to match the scoreboard.
While Rangers now fall to sixth, Livingston rise to third place, behind Hibernian by goal difference.
And Rangers could face more difficulties afterwards, as line judge Calum Spence needed medical attention for a head wound caused by an object that was apparently thrown from the stands.
Gerrard made eight changes from line-up that swept the Ayrs in the Betfred Cup semi-finals.
Holt, on the other hand, came out with the same starting eleven that kicked off a point against Hearts last week. Surely, he felt satisfied by the way his team, well organized, suffocated his rivals at all times.
While the Rangers dominated possession, Livingston created more danger when they had the ball.
In fact, they should have scored after two minutes of the game, when Menga's first intervention left Scot Pittman alone against Allan McGregor, but the midfielder kicked deflected and wasted the great occasion.
A blunt foul shot from Keaghan Jacobs collided with Captain Craig Halkett, but his defensive partner Alan Lithgow only made a deflected shot at the back post.
The Rangers took control, but seemed to have more hope than faith in their game in the final third.
Alfredo Morelos really believed he should have had a penalty on the fifteen-minute signal, as he and Steven Lawless stumbled, but referee Steven Thomson ignored the Colombian’s claims.
The Rangers only led two shots to goal the first half, but Ibrox’s first goalkeeper, Liam Kelly, had no problem with Lassana Coulibaly’s header and docile striker Ovie Ejaria.
While Livi's first act the 34th minute could have been against the race, no one can deny that they deserved it just for their effort.
Again, the Rangers were unable to deal with Jacobs's hard-fought move.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher passed the ball to Scott Robinson, who remained calm to choose Menga for a simple ending.
Gerrard performed on pause when changing Coulibaly for Ryan Kent. The change had a virtually immediate impact, as the winger made a pass to Morelos, even though the imposing Kelly raced in the lockdown.
However, the Livingstons continued to capture the attention of visitors by playing exactly as they like, with Lithgow and Halkett making long passes, one after another.
Holt's side may have yielded direction to the final stages, but McGregor stood firm to reject Jacobs before Lithgow made a deflected shot from the corner.
Ranger substitute Glenn Middleton still asked for one last penalty for a dispute with Jacobs, but again Thomson turned a blind eye.
Almanac: the inventor of the Geiger counter
And now a "Sunday Morning" Almanac page: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago and counting... the day when the physique of the future, Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger, was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method to detect and measure radioactivity, an invention that eventually gave rise to the device known as “Geiger accountant”.
The Geiger counter, which has been a pillar of science since then, also became a pile of pop culture, as in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado", starring Roy Rogers and Dale Evans, apparently failed cowboy physicists:
Man: "What is this tomorrow?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger counter, which is used to find radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
When you put on these headphones, you can listen to the effects of atoms emanating from radioactivity to minerals."
Evans: "I'm sure it's erupting now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, a few days after his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name is still alive.
A new vaccine against cancer can teach the immune system to “see” malignant cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” and eliminate malignant cells
Vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells as part of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient and altering them in the laboratory
You can “see” a protein common to many cancers and then reinject them
A test vaccine is showing promising results in patients with diverse cancer types.
A woman treated with the vaccine, who teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells, saw the ovarian cancer she was suffering from for more than 18 months disappear.
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient, altering them in the laboratory to be able to "see" a protein present in many types of cancer (HER2) and, later, re-inject them.
According to Professor Jay Berzofsky of the National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland (U.S.): "The results that we have obtained suggest that we have a promising vaccine."
The HER2 protein “favors the growth of various types of cancer”, including breast, ovarian, lung and colorectal, explained Professor Berzofsky.
A similar approach consisting of extracting immune cells from patients and “teaching” these cells how to treat cancer cells has worked in the treatment of a type of leukaemia.
Kanye West embarked on a pro-Trump diatribe with a MAGA cap following her performance on the SNL program.
It didn't end well
Kanye West was shouted in the studio during Saturday Night Live after a chaotic performance congratulating U.S. President Donald Trump and said he would stand in the 2020 election.
After his third song of the night, titled Ghost Town, in which he wore a Make America Great Again cap, he began criticizing Democrats and reiterating his support for Trump.
"Whenever I talk to white people I'm told: "How can you like Trump if he's a racist?
"Well, if I worried about racism, I would have left the United States a long time ago," he said.
The SNL program began with a parody of Matt Damon, in which the Hollywood star mocked Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee over allegations of sexual assault filed by Christine Blasey Ford.
Although it did not air, comedian Chris Rock uploaded West's disapproval recording on social media.
It's unclear whether Rock intended to ridicule West with publication.
However, West has complained to the public that he was not well treated behind the scenes because of his cap.
"They threatened me behind the scenes.
They told me not to go out with that cap on.
I was harassed.
And then they say I'm in a lower position," he said, according to Washington Examiner.
West continued: "Do you want to see the lower position?" while saying he would put on Superman's layer because that's how they couldn't tell him what he has to do. "Do you want the world to move forward?
Try to love.”
His comments provoked shouting from the public at least twice and the members of the program team seemed embarrassed, according to Variety, who had a reporter who explained in the publication: "The entire study was completely silent."
West was taken as a last-minute substitute for Ariana Grande, whose e.g. Rapper Mac Miller had died a few days ago.
West left a good part of the audience perplexed with the interpretation of the song I Love it, disguised as Perrier’s bottle.
West obtained the support of the head of the conservative TPUSA group, Candace Turner, who tweeted: "To one of the most courageous spirits: THANK YOU FOR HIGHLIGHTING BETWEEN THE CROWD."
But debate show presenter Karen Hunter tweeted that West was simply "being who he is and that he's absolutely wonderful."
"But I've chosen not to reward someone (buying their music or clothing or supporting their "art") that I think adopts and spreads an ideology that harms my community.
He's free.
And us too," he added.
Ahead of the program, the rapper announced on Twitter that his name had been changed and that he was now "the being formerly known as Kanye West."
He's not the first artist to change his name and follows in the footsteps of Diddy, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
His rapper colleague, Snoop Dogg, was formerly called Snoop Lion and the defunct music legend Prince changed his name to a symbol and, later, the artist formerly known as Prince.
Accusation of murder in attempted stabbing at Belfast restaurant
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder following the stabbing of a man Friday east Belfast.
According to police, the incident took place in Ballyhackamore.
The defendant is expected to appear in front of Belfast court on Monday.
Prosecutors will review the charges.
Game of Thrones star Kit Harington denounces toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for portraying the role of swordsman Jon Snow in HBO’s violent medieval fantasy series Game of Thrones.
However, the actor, 31, has denounced the stereotype of virile hero, stating that these roles imply that young boys often believe that they must be strong to be respected.
In an interview with The Sunday Times Culture magazine, Kit said he believed that “something had gone wrong” and that it questioned how to tackle the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who has recently married Game of Thrones co-stager Rose Leslie, also 31, has admitted that he feels a lot of determination to face this problem.
“Personally, now I’m wondering where we were wrong in terms of masculinity,” he said.
“What have we taught men during their development in relation to the problem that we are experiencing now?”
According to Kit, television can have some responsibility for the increase in toxic masculinity thanks to its virile characters.
He added: "Which is innate and what is acquired?
What is taught in television and on the streets that makes young boys feel that they must have this facet of virility?
I think it's one of the big questions of our time: how do we change it?
“There’s certainly something wrong with young boys.”
In the interview, he also admitted that he wouldn’t make any prequels to Game of Thrones when the end of the series is scheduled for next summer, saying he’s fed up with battles and horses.
From November, Kit will star in a replenishment of Sam Shepard’s True West, which tells the story of a film producer and his brother, who is a thief.
The actor recently revealed that getting to know his partner Rose is the best he has been provided by Game of Thrones.
"I met my wife in the series, so she gave me my future family and my life onwards," he said.
Rose played Ygritte, the love of the character Kit, Jon Snow, in the Emmy-winning fantasy series.
The couple married in June 2018 to Leslie's family estate in Scotland.
HIV/AIDS: China notifies 14% increase in new cases
China has announced a 14% increase in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
There are more than 820,000 people affected in the country, according to health agents.
Only in the second quarter of 2018, 40,000 new cases were declared.
The vast majority of new cases are of sexual transmission, unlike what happened in the past.
HIVO traditionally propagated rapidly in some regions of China due to infected blood transfusions.
However, the number of people contracting HIV for this cause has been reduced by almost zero, according to the statements of Chinese health agents at a conference in Yunnan province.
Year after year, however, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased 100,000 people.
The transmission of HIV sexually is a serious problem for China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality collapsed in China in 1997, but discrimination of the LGBT community is common.
Taking into account the conservative values of the country, studies estimate that between 70 and 90% of men who have homosexual relationships will end up marrying a woman.
Many transmissions of diseases are due to a lack of adequate precautions in sexual intercourse.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to HIV medication as part of an effort to tackle the problem.
Maxine Waters denies that an employee has leaked data from Republican Party senators and dismantles “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories”
U.S. representative Maxine Waters on Saturday denounced the accusations that a member of her staff had published personal information from three U.S. Republican senators on legislators' Wikipedia pages.
The Los Angeles Democrat claimed that the demands were driven by critics and far-right websites.
"Lies, lies and more despicable lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
Supposedly, the published information contained the addresses and phone numbers of U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham, from South Carolina, and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information appeared online on Thursday and was posted by someone unknown at the Capitol during a session of the Senate commission on allegations of sexual abuse against Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh.
The information was leaked after three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address that identifies the source of publications was related to the Waters office and revealed information from a member of Waters staff, the Capitol denounced.
"This baseless denunciation is completely false and a pure lie," Waters continued.
"The member of my staff whose identity, personal data and security have been compromised due to these false and fraudulent allegations, was not responsible in any way for this leak of information.
This baseless denunciation is completely false and a pure invention."
Waters' statement generated criticism online quickly, including that of former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"This denial is full of outrage," Fleischer wrote.
"This suggests he doesn't have the temperament to be a member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they haven't done, they shouldn't get angry.
It doesn't have to be defiant.
He doesn't have to question the accuser's motives.
He must remain calm and serenity.”
Fleischer seemed to compare Waters' reaction to criticism from Judge Kavanaugh's Democrats, whom critics accused of appearing too angry during Thursday's session.
Omar Navarro, a Republican nominee who was running to overthrow Waters in mid-term elections, also expressed his opinion on Twitter.
"Important if it's true," he tweeted.
In his statement, Waters said his office had alerted the relevant authorities and security forces of such fraudulent statements.
"We will make sure that the offenders are disclosed, he continued, "and that they will be legally responsible for all the actions they have carried out and that they have harmed each and every one of the members of my staff."
Critique by Johnny English Strikes Again: Rowan Atkinson’s Limited Spy Parody
It is already a tradition to look for references to Brexit in any new film with a British approach, and this revival of Johnny English’s action-comedy parody franchise, which was born in 2003 with Johnny English and reappeared in 2011 with Johnny English Reborn, is not an exception.
This humorous self-satire about how despicable we are will be a new export opportunity for the country?
In any case, the incompetent Johnny English has been able to renew his license again for the second time to twist everything. His name indicates more than anything else that it is a comic creation designed for non-English-speaking territories with a tradition of going to the cinema.
Evidently, it's the stupid secret agent who, despite his rare pretensions of liquefied glamour, has some Clouseau, a point from Mr. Bean and a touch of this guy who brings a single note to the theme of Carros de foc at the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics.
Originally, it was based on the international and mysterious traveler Atkinson representing Barclaycard's now-forgotten television commercials, where he sowed chaos everywhere.
There are one or two good times on this last JE trip.
I loved the Johnny English who was approaching a helicopter dressed in medieval armor and the propeller’s blades were ringing on his helmet.
Atkinson’s gift for physical comedy is seen, but the humor seems quite loose and strangely superfluous, especially since “serious” cinematographic brands such as 007 and Mission Impossible now include comedy as an ingredient.
Humor seems destined for children rather than adults and, for me, Johnny English’s absurd adventures are not as ingenious or focused as the silent gags of the character Mr. Bean representing Atkinson.
The repeatedly topically premise now is that Britain is in serious trouble.
A hacker has infiltrated Britain’s super-secrete web spy network and revealed the identities of all British agents on the ground, prompting dismay from the service agent, an unfortunately small role for Kevin Eldon.
It is the last resort for a prime minister who is a pretentious and harassed figure, who already suffers a total collapse of lack of political popularity: Emma Thompson gives the best of herself with this calced character of Teresa-May, but the script doesn’t get any more.
His intelligence advisors inform him that, as each active spy is jeopardized, he will have to recover a retiree one.
And this includes the awkward Johnny English, now employed as a teacher in some luxury institution, but teaching unofficial classes on how to become an undercover agent, with some good gags, such as when English offers an espionage academy that looks like a rock school.
English is quickly returned to Whitehall for an emergency meeting with his sequacious sufferer Bough, a role again representing Ben Miller.
Now Bough is a married man, tied to the commander of a submarine, an enthusiastic role, in which Vicki Pepperdine is a little wasted.
Thus, Batman and Robin when things get very ugly in Her Majesty’s secret service come into action once again and find Olga Kurylenko the spectacular “femme fatale” Ophelia Bulletova.
Meanwhile, the Prime Minister falls dangerously understood the charismatic technological bilionaire who claims he can solve Britain’s computer problems: the sinister Jason Volta, represented by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of ridiculous madness: disguised as waiters, they set fire to a French fast-food restaurant, sowing chaos by smuggling aboard Volta’s luxury yacht and English causes great confusion when trying to use a virtual reality helmet to familiarize themselves with the interior of Volta’s house.
All the stops have certainly been extracted from this last sequence, but with all the simplicity and noise it presents, everything seems like a children’s program.
Quite normal things.
As with the other Johnny English films, it has not been helpful to think about whether the film industry has given Rowan Atkinson a role to do justice to his talent.
The Labour Party denies that it is devising a plan for the Britons to have a four-day working week and to cover five
Jeremy Corbyn's Labor Party will have to consider a radical plan according to which the Britons will work four days a weekly and charge five.
Supposedly, the party wants the heads of the companies to spend the savings obtained with the revolution of artificial intelligence (AI) to the workers by giving them one more day of celebration.
Employees would enjoy a three-day weekend, but they would receive the same salary.
According to the sources, the idea would fit with the economic agenda of the parties and the plans to tilt the country in favor of workers.
The four-day week-labor change has the support of the Trades Union Congress as a method for workers to take advantage of economic change.
An experienced source from the Labor Party told The Sunday Times: “A review of politics is expected to be announced before the end of the year.
It won’t be overnight, but a four-day working week is an aspiration that fits the party’s prospect of balancing the economy in favor of the worker and the party’s general industrial strategy.”
The Labour Party would not be the first to support this idea, as the Green Party promised a four-day working week during the 2017 general election campaign.
However, the Labor Party does not support this aspiration jointly today.
“A four-day working week is not party politics and the party is not considering it.”
Shadow Minister John McDonnell used last week's party conference to detail his view of a socialist revolution in the economy.
McDonnell expressed his determination to restore the power of "anonymous directors" and "speculators" to utility companies.
Shadow minister's plans also imply that shareholders of water companies might not recover the entire investment, as the Labour government could apply "deductions" for the crimes it perceives.
It has also confirmed plans to place workers on company boards and create inclusive-owned funds to deliver 10% of the shares of private sector firms to employees, who hope to pocket annual dividends worth up to £500.
Lindsey Graham, John Kennedy explains in "60 Minutes" if Kavanaugh's FBI investigation could make them change their mind.
FBI's investigation of accusations against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has delayed the final vote of his nomination for the Supreme Court for at least a week, and has arisen the question about whether the agency's discoveries could influence some Republican senator in withdrawing support.
In an interview to be released on Sunday, 60-Minute correspondent Scott Pelley asked Republican Senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham if the FBI could bring to light anything that made them change their mind.
Kennedy was more open than his South Carolina colleague.
"I'm talking seriously," Kennedy said.
"At the session I said I had spoken to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him when everything happened and the accusations were made public, and I asked him if he had done so.
It was firm, determined and safe."
But Graham's vote seems engraved on fire.
"I have my opinion on Brett Kavanaugh and I would lodge an explosive accusation," he said.
"Dr. Ford, I don't know what's happened, but I know this: Brett flatly denied it," Graham added referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
"And none of those you appoint will be able to verify it.
He is 36 years old.
"I don't see anything changing again."
What is the Global Citizen Festival? Have you done anything to reduce poverty?
New York will host this Saturday the Global Citizen Festival, an annual musical event, which presents an absolutely impressive star program, carrying out an equally impressive mission: to eradicate poverty in the world.
In the seventh edition this year, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of thousands of people gather in the Great Lawn area of Central Park not only to enjoy performances such as those of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the global goal of the event of ending extreme poverty in the world before 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to end poverty by increasing the number of people helping to actively combat it.
To receive a free ticket for the event (unless someone wants to pay for a VIP ticket), attendees had to do a series of tasks or "actions", such as volunteering, sending an email to a world leader, making a phone call or any other activity to raise awareness about the goal of ending poverty.
What successes has Global Citizen achieved now that you have 12 years left to reach your goal?
The idea of rewarding people with a free concert is a good way to persuade her to demand a call to action or just another case of what we call "clictivism" (people who think make a difference to sign an online petition or submit a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen claims to have registered more than 19 million "actions" of its sympathizers, which have helped to achieve several objectives.
It declares that these actions have helped stimulate world leaders to announce commitments and policies comparable to more than 37,000 million USD, which will have influenced the lives of more than 2,250 million people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group mentioned 390 commitments and announcements derived from its shares, of which at least €10,000 million has already been disbursed or raised.
The group estimates that for the moment the protected funds have exerted a direct impact on almost 649 million people around the world.
Some of the major commitments include The Power of Nutrition foundation, a UK-based association of investors and filmmakers, which is committed to "helping children grow as much as possible" and promises to send Rwanda £35 million to help combat malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
"Thanks for the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and members of Global Citizens, like you, we can turn the social injustice of malnutrition into a footer in history," The Power of Nutrition Ambassador Tracey Ullman told the crowd during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also explained that after more than 5,000 actions calling on the United Kingdom to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government announced the funding of a project, The Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children with nutrition interventions.
In response to one of the P+Fs on his website asking "what makes you think we can end extreme poverty?"
Global Citizen replied: "It will be a long and hard road. Sometimes, we'll fall and fail.
But as the great civil rights and anti-apartheid movements above, we will succeed, because together we are stronger.”
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe are some of the artists who will perform at this year's event in New York, who will present Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The U.S. could use the Navy as "blocking" to hinder Russian energy exports (Secretary of the Interior)
Washington can, "if it is necessary" to resort to the Navy to prevent Russian energy from hitting markets, including those in the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke has revealed, according to the Washington Examiner.
Zinke has denounced that Russia's intervention in Syria, mainly where it operates at the invitation of the legitimate government, is a pretext to explore new energy markets.
"I believe that the reason they are in the Middle East is their willingness to negotiate with energy, as they already do in Eastern Europe, Europe's southern tummy," he said.
According to the officer, there are ways and means to address this issue.
"The U.S. has the capacity, along with the Navy, to ensure that the seaways are open and, if necessary, for the blockade, to prevent its energy from entering the market," he said.
Zinke addressed those attending the event held by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit group that defines itself as the "energy consumer voice" in the U.S.
He compared Washington's strategies for negotiating with Russia and Iran, noting that they are indeed equal.
"The economic option in Iran and Russia consists, more or less, in taking advantage of and replacing fuels," he said, while indicating that Russia has only one resource, since its economy depends on fossil fuels.
Declarations come while the Trump administration has been on a mission to boost the export of its liquefied natural gas to Europe, replacing Russia, the most economical option for European consumers.
To this end, the officers of the Trump administration, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to persuade Germany to get out of the "inadequate" Nord Stream gas pipeline project , which according to Trump, made Berlin "captive" of Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly insisted that the Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which has cost $11,000 million and which will double the existing gas pipeline capacity to 110,000 million cubic meters, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's fervent opposition to the project is simply due to economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
I believe that we share the opinion that energy cannot be a tool to pressure and that consumers must be able to choose suppliers, declared Russia's Energy Minister Alexander Novak after a meeting with U.S. Energy Department Secretary Rick Perry, held in Moscow in September.
The U.S. stance has provoked a negative response from Germany, which has reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's main organisation, FDI (Federation of German Industries), has appealed the U.S. to remove EU energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"I have a big problem when a third state interferes with our energy supply," stated Dieter Kempf, director of BDI (Federation of German Industries) following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren has said she will “closely think about” her candidacy for the presidency in 2020.
Senator for Massachusetts Elizabeth Warren said on Saturday that she would “carefully think” her candidacy for the presidency after the mid-term elections.
During a municipal meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed that he would consider his candidacy.
"It's time for women to go to Washington and fix the government's divide, for women to hold high positions," she said.
“After November 6, I will take my candidacy for president seriously.”
Warren intervened on President Donald Trump during the municipal meeting and claimed he "was taking the country in the wrong direction."
"I'm frankly worried about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy," she said.
Warren has been frank in her criticism of Trump and his candidate for the Supreme Court, Brett Kavanaugh.
In a tweet on Friday, Warren said, "Of course you need an FBI investigation ahead of the election."
However, a post that was published on Thursday showed that a majority of Warren's own voters believed it shouldn't show up by 2020.
Fifty-eight percent of Massachusetts' "likely" constituents have said the senator shouldn't show up, according to the Suffolk University Political Research Center/Boston Globe survey.
Thirty-two percent would support the candidacy.
The survey revealed wider support for the candidacy of former Governor Deval Patrick, with 38% in favor of a possible candidacy and $48 percent against.
Other high-profile names among Democrats that were taken into account in relation to a possible candidacy for 2020 include Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
Biden said he'd officially reveal his decision in January, according to Associated Press.
Sarah Palin cites TEPT-Track Palin-affected at Donald Trump rally
Track Palin (26) spent a year in Iraq after enlisting on September 11, 2007
On Monday night he was arrested and charged over a domestic violence incident
"With what's been happening my own son since he came back, I can understand other families suffering from PTSD ramifications and some of the emotional wounds with which our soldiers return, "he explained to the public at a Donald Trump rally in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called the arrest "the elephant in the room" and said the following of his son and other war veterans: "they come back a little differently, come back harded, come back wondering if there is respect for what his fellow soldiers and pilots, as well as any other member of the army, have donated to the country."
He was arrested Monday in Wasilla, Alaska, and charges of domestic violence against a woman are charged, with a statement for domestic violence and possession of weapons under the influence of alcohol, according to Dan Bennett, spokesman for the Wasilla Police Department.
18 states and D.C. support the challenge to a new asylum policy
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia support the legal challenge to a new U.S. policy that denies asylum to victims fleeing gangs or domestic violence
Representatives from 18 states and the district presented a friend curiae Friday in Washington to back an asylum seeker defying politics, according to NBC News.
The full name of the Grace vs Sessions case lawsuit that the American Civil Liberties Union filed in August against federal policy has not been disclosed.
He said his partner "and the children of the violent gang member" assaulted her, but U.S. officers denied their request for asylum on July 20.
She was arrested in Texas.
Lawyers from states supporting Grace have described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, where a large number of asylum seekers are generated in the U.S., as countries facing widespread gang problems and domestic violence.
U.S. new asylum policy revoked a 2014 decision by the Immigration Appeals Board, allowing undocumented immigrants to flee domestic violence to seek asylum.
Columbia District Attorney General Karl Racine stated on Friday that the new policy "ignores decades of state, federal and international legislation."
Federal laws require that asylum claims be awarded according to the particular facts and circumstances of the lawsuit, and this prohibition violates this principle, according to amicus curiae.
Lawyers also argued in the report that policy denying the entry of immigrants harms the U.S. economy, as they are more likely to become employers and "provide necessary jobs."
In June, Attorney-General Jeff Sessions ordered immigration judges not to grant more asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and gangs.
"Asylum is available to those people who leave their country out of persecution or fear on the grounds of race, religion, nationality or membership of a specific social group or political opinion," Sessions told its June 11 announcement of that policy.
Asylum has never sought to alleviate all the problems, not even the most serious, that people face every day around the world.
Desperate rescue efforts in Palu as human losses multiply in a sprint to find survivors
For survivors, the situation is increasingly desperate.
"The atmosphere is very tense," 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma said while reassuring her son with a fever at an evacuation center in the destroyed city of Palu.
"Every minute, an ambulance carries bodies.
There is a lack of drinking water.”
Residents have been seen returning to their homes destroyed to catch flooded belongings and trying to save what they can.
There are hundreds injured, and hospitals, damaged by magnitude 7.5 quake, are saturated.
Some of those injured, including Dwi Haris, who has fractured their shoulders, are located outside Palu's military hospital, as patients are treated outside due to heavy shocks that are repeated.
Tears denied his eyes as he explained how the violent earthquake shook the room on the fifth floor of the hotel where he met his wife and daughter.
“There wasn’t time to save us.
He was trapped in the ruins of the wall, I think," Haris explained to Associated Press, adding that his family had attended a wedding in the village.
“I heard my wife ask for help crying and then I didn’t heard her anymore.
I don't know what has happened to her and my daughter.
I hope they're fine.'
U.S. ambassador accuses China of harassment with "propaganda announcements."
A week after an official Chinese publication published a four-page advertisement in an U.S. newspaper promoting the mutual advantages of U.S
President Donald Trump addressed China Daily's payment supplement last Wednesday at The Des Moines Register, Yowa's best-selling newspaper, after accusing China of getting involved in the U.S. Congress election on November 6, an indictment that China denies.
Trump's indictment that Beijing was trying to get involved in the U.S. election marked what U.S. officials have told Reuters it's a new phase of Washington's campaign to intensify pressure on China.
Although it is normal for foreign governments to insert ads to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently engaged in a trade war that is intensifying and that has seen tariffs on mutual imports rise.
China’s vengeful tariffs at the beginning of the trade war aimed to punish exporters of states, such as Iowa, that supported Trump’s Republican Party, according to Chinese and U.S. experts.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. ambassador to China and former governor of Iowa for a long time, a major exporter of agricultural products to China, claimed that Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers, and businesses.
China, Branstad wrote in an op-ed in The Des Moines Register of The Sunday: "It's redoubling harassment with the publication of propaganda announcements in our free press."
“By disseminatinging their propaganda, the Chinese government uses America’s beloved tradition of freedom of expression and the free press by inserting a paid advertisement into The Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
"On the other hand, at the kiosk below here in Beijing, you'll find few disagreeing voices and you won't see any real reflection of the disparate views that Chinese citizens may have about China's worrying economic trajectory, given that the media is dominated by the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's most prominent newspapers dodged the offer to publish" his article, but did not say the publication's name.
Republicans remove women's voters ahead of mid-term elections with Kavanaugh disaster, analysts warn
As many major Republicans support and defend Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh, who is facing several lawsuits for sexual assault, analysts have warned that they will see a negative response, especially among women, in the upcoming mid-term elections.
Emotions surrounding this case are extremely high and most Republicans already officially show that they want to take a step forward with a vote.
These things can't be undone," explained Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Syracuse University in The Hill for an article that was published on Saturday.
Reeher said he doubts that Senator Jeff Flake's (R-Arizona's last-minute pressures) for an FBI investigation to take place will be enough to calm angry voters.
"Women won't forget what happened yesterday, neither tomorrow nor in November," said Karine Jean-Pierre, chief adviser and national spokesperson for the progressive group MoveOn on Friday, according to the Washington, D.C
On Friday morning, Protestants shouted "november is coming!" (November is approaching) as they demonstrated in the Senate lobby while Republicans controlling the Judiciary Committee decided to continue Kavanagugh's nomination despite testimony from Dr Christine Blasey Ford, according to Mic.
"Democrats' enthusiasm and motivation will be left out of the chart," said Stu Rothenberg, an independent political analyst at the news site.
"People say they've already been tall; it's true.
But they could have been more so, especially among women's voters from suburbs and younger voters, aged 18 to 29, who often don't vote while they don't like the president.'
Even before Ford's public testimony, where he detailed his demands for sexual assault on the Supreme Court candidate, analysts suggested that a negative response could occur if Republicans pressed with confirmation.
"This has become a mess for the GOP," said Michael Steele, former president of the Republican National Committee, early last week, according to NBC News.
“It’s not just about the board’s vote or the final vote, or whether Kavanaugh sits on the defendants’ bench. It also has to do with how the Republicans have handled this issue and how they’ve dealt with it,” said Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA, a group that helps elect Democrats on the news channel.
However, the Americans appear to be divided between those who believe in Ford’s wake and Kavanaugh’s testimonies, with the scale that leans slightly towards the last one.
A new YouGov survey shows that 41 percent of respondents unquestionably or probablely believe in Ford's testimony, while 35 percent claim to unquestionably or probable Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38% said he believed Kavanaugh had lied likely or undoubtedly during his testimony, while 30 percent claimed the same from Ford.
After Flake’s pressure, the FBI is currently investigating lawsuits filed by Ford and at least another whistleblower, Deborah Ramirez, according to The Guardian.
Ford testified before the Senate Judiciary Under Oath last week that Kavanaugh had assaulted him under the influence of alcohol when he was 17.
Ramirez denounces that the Supreme Court candidate showed him his genitals at a party while they were studying at Yale in the 80's.
The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to create a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the web is launching a new company that aims to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest technology legend project, Inrupt, is a company that is based on Berners-Lee's open-score platform Solid.
The Solid platform allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people can access certain information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that Inrupt’s hidden intention was to “master the world.”
"We have to do it now," he said of the start-up company.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses the technology of the Solid platform to allow users to create their own “personal online data warehouse” or a POD.
It can contain contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It’s such as Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify. They’re all available in one browser at a time.
What is exclusive to the personal online data warehouse is that the user decides who can access certain information.
The company calls it “personal empowerment through data.”
Inrupt’s idea, according to the company’s CEO, John Bruce, is for the company to integrate appropriate resources, processes and aptitudes so that the Solid platform is available to everyone.
The company is currently made up of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform acquired by IBM, some staff developers hired to work on the project and a community of volunteer encoders.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on Inrupt’s website.
Berners-Lee said that no one on his team is talking to "Facebook and Google about whether it is necessary to introduce a radical change where all business models are completely emptied during the night.
"We'ren't asking them for permission."
In a Saturday’s publication on the Medium, Berners-Lee wrote that “Inrupt’s mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new website integrated into the Solid platform.”
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate over net neutrality.
Even during the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee will continue to be the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the World Wide Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this new era of the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebration of the Victory Cleric of the I GM
England’s only cleric who won a Victory Cross during World War I as a combatant has been honored in his hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant colonel Bernard Vann won the award on September 29, 1818 for the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, he was shot down by a sniper four days later and never knew that he had won the most important British military honour.
His two grandchildren uncovered a commemorative tombstone at a parade on Saturday in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, said it was "magnificently symbolic" that the tombstone showed up exactly 100 years after his grandfather's award-winning feat.
According to The London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion down the Saint-Quentin Canal "through very thick fog and under the intense fire of cannons and machine guns."
He later ran towards the line of fire and, with the "maximum bravery" brought the row forward before throwing himself over a one-handed cannon and leaving three members of the detachment stunned.
Lieutenant colonel was shot by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the war ended.
Michael Vann (72) said that his grandfather’s actions were a feat he could never match, which gives you a sense of humility.
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also left a wreath after the parade, led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said that he felt very honored to be part of the parade and added that the value of an authentic hero is demonstrated by support that he will receive from many people.
MMA fans who ran out of sleep all night to see Bellator 206, saw Peppa Pig in her place
Imagine that you stay awake all night to see Bellator 206 and be denied seeing the most important event.
San Jose’s poster contained 13 fights, including six of the main card and had to be seen live all night in the UK on Channel 5.
At 6:00, when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing for the showdown, UK viewers were stunned when programming changed to Peppa Pig.
Some were disgusted to have spent the night awake until dawn to see the fight.
A follower on Twitter described the change by cartoons as "a kind of vomiting joke."
"Government regulations say that at 6:00 this content isn't appropriate, so they switched to children's programming," said Dave Schwartz, Bellator's vice president of marketing and communication, when asked about the broadcast.
"Pep the piglet, yes."
Bellator Chairman Scott Coker said they would work on programming to include UK viewers in the future.
"I think that when I think about repetition, I think it can work," Coker said.
"But there it's six o'clock on a Sunday morning and we won't be able to solve it until Sunday at our time, Monday at your own.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, when I changed the channel, there were many messages that came and went, and not all of them were nice.
We tried to solve it, we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn't like that. It was a government problem.
I promise that it won't happen again.
We’ll leave it in five fights, rather than six as we normally do. We tried to show it again for the followers, but it ended.
It's an unfortunate situation.'
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt “inferior” about sexuality
Olympic jumper Tom Daley claims to have grown up feeling inferior to the rest of the people because of his sexuality, but that gave him the motivation to succeed.
The 24-year-old athlete has stated that he didn't realise until he went to high school that not everyone was like him.
On Radio 4’s Desert Island Discs program, presented by Lauren Laverne, he said he talked about gay rights to give “hope” to other people.
He also said that being a father had subtracted the importance of winning the Olympics.
The usual presenter of the long-term program, Kirsty Young, has taken a few months of sick leave.
Daley, who appeared as a castaway on Laverne's first program, said he felt "less than" others because "it wasn't socially acceptable as boys and girls."
“To this day, those feelings of inferiority and difference have been real things that have given me the power and strength to succeed.”
He wanted to prove that it was "something," he said, so as not to disappoint anyone when his sexuality was discovered.
The two-time winner of the Olympic bronze medal has become a high-profile promoter of the LGBT movement and has used his image this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to ask more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said that he had spoken because he felt fortunate to be able to live openly without ramifications and that he wanted to give “hope” to other people.
The three-time world champion explained that he had fallen in love with a man, the American filmmaker, Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013, "it took me by surprise."
Last year, Daley married the Oscar winner, 20 older than him, but says the age difference has never been a problem.
According to him, when you spend therefore when you are young (he participated in his first Olympic Games when he was 14 and his father died of cancer three years later), it is difficult to find someone of the same age who has experienced similar highs and lows.
The couple were parents in June of a kid whom they named Robert Ray Black-Daley. According to Daley, "their entire perspective" has changed.
"If I had wondered last year, all I needed was to win an Olympic gold," he said.
"There are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son is named the same as his father, Robert, who died in 2011, at the age of 40 due to brain cancer.
According to Daley, his father did not accept that he had to die and one of the last things he asked was if they already had the tickets for London 2012, because he wanted to be in the front row.
"I couldn't tell him 'father, you won't be there to occupy the front row,'" he said.
"While holding his hand, he stopped breathing and it wasn't until then that he was already dead, that I finally accepted that it wasn't invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
"I knew that was what I had dreamed of all my life: jumping in front of a crowd at home at the Olympics. There was no better feeling," he declared.
It also inspired the choice of his first song (Proud de Heather Small), which he had listened to while preparing for the Olympics and which still made his goose bump.
Desert Island Discs is broadcast on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 BST.
Worsen Mickelson showed up at the Ryder Cup on Saturday
U.S. Phil Mickelson will break a record on Sunday by playing his 47th Ryder Cup match, but he'll have to change his fitness if he doesn't want to become an unhappy milestone.
Mickelson, who will participate in the biennial event to break a record for the 12th time, was unveiled by Captain Jim Furyk for Saturday’s “fourballs” and “foursomes” modalities.
Instead of being at the center of the action, as has usually been for the United States, the winner of five main tournaments divides his time between being an entertainer and working on his game on the field, in the hope of rectifying what hurts him.
He has never done the most direct drivers, not even at the height of his career, but the 48-year-old player is not the ideal option for the difficult trajectory of Le Golf National, where the longer usually punishes deflected blows.
And if his own career is not daunting enough, Mickelson, in the ninth game on Sunday, he will face the precise British Open champion, Francesco Molinari, who has teamed up to rookie Tommy Fleetwood to win all four games this week.
If the Americans, four points down at the start of all 12 individual matches, start strong, Mickelson's match could become absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in this man, unable to say much more.
"He fully understood his role for that day, gave me a palm in the back and surrounded me with his arm as he told me that he would be ready the next day," Furyk said.
"He has a lot of confidence in himself.
It's a hall of fame and has contributed a lot to these teams previously and this week.
I probably didn't imagine him playing two matches.
I imagined more, but that’s how I trained and that’s how we thought we had to move forward.
He wants to be there, just like anyone else.”
On Sunday, Mickelson will surpass Nick Faldo's record for the highest number of Ryder Cup matches played.
It can mean the end of a career in the Ryder Cup that hasn't matched his individual record.
Mickelson has obtained 18 victories, 20 defeats and 7 draws, although Furyk has stated that his presence has contributed intangibles to the team.
"It's funny, sarcastic, joking, he likes to make fun of people and he's a great partner for the team," he explained.
"I think the younger players also had fun messing with him this week, which has been fun to see.
It’s much more about his game.”
European captain Thomas Bjorn knows the advantage can soon fade
Thomas Bjorn, the European captain, knows from experience that starting with a considerable advantage the individual duels of the last day of Ryder Cup can easily become a tough day.
The Danish made his debut in 1997 in Valderrama, when the team captained by Seve Ballesteros had a five-point advantage over the American team, but in the end they got the victory by the minimum, 14.5-13.5.
“You keep reminding you that we had a great advantage in Valderrama; we had a great advantage in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but narrowly,” said Bjorn, in the image, after seeing the victory of the 2018 team by 5-3 both Friday and yesterday, which leaves the score at 10-6 at Le Golf National.
History has taught me and all team members that it's not over yet.
Tomorrow you have to go to every machine.
You have to get out and do everything right.
This doesn't end until the points get on the scoreboard.
We have a goal, trying to win the trophy, and we have to stay focused to do so.
I have already said, I focus on the 12 players we have in the team, but I also keep in mind who our rivals are: the best players in the world.”
Delighted with the performance of his players on a demanding golf course, Bjorn added: “I’ll never rush with that.
It's going to be different tomorrow.
Tomorrow the individual performance will make a difference and change everything.
It's great to be there with a partner when everything's going well, but individual duels test your ability to play golf.
That's the message you have to convey to the players, get the best out of yourself tomorrow.
At this moment, you leave your partner and he has to come out and get the best of himself too.”
In opposition to Bjourn, rival captain Jim Furyk expects his players to perform better individually than as a team, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who added three points out of four.
Furyk knows both sides of the coin over last-day comebacks, as he was part of Brookline’s winning team before ending up losing in the “Medinah Miracle” that Europe won.
"I remember exactly all the words," he replied to the question of how did Ben Crenshaw, the captain of 1999, his players motivated before the last day.
“Tomorrow we have 12 important matches, but we want to start on a good footing, as was seen in Brookline and Medinah.
When there is a team that is inspired, a lot of pressure is generated in these central duels.
We have prepared the alignment according to the circumstances and in the best way to try to do magic tomorrow.”
Thomas is challenged to trike the comeback against Rory McIlroy in the main duel, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter as in the rest of Europeans in the first half of the order.
“I’ve chosen this order because I think it covers all the possibilities,” Bjorn says about his selection for individual clashes.
Germany's new warship, postponed again
The new frigate of the German Navy should have been completed in 2014 to replace the old warships of the Cold War era, but it won't do so at least until next year as a result of defective systems and an increase in cost, according to local media.
The completion of “Rheinland-Pfalz”, the main shirt of the new Baden-Württemberg class frigates, has been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit newspaper, which cites a military spokesman.
The ship should have joined the Navy in 2014, but post-construction problems have marked the deadlines for this ambitious project.
The four Baden-Württemberg-class ships that the Navy commissioned in 2007 will replace the former Bremen-class frigates.
It is believed that it will include a powerful cannon, a set of anti-aircraft missiles and anti-shiels, as well as technology against furtive actions, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Other outstanding features include longer periods between maintenance: it should be possible to use the new frigates for up to two years without going through domestic ports.
However, these continuous delays imply that cutting-edge ships, which were expected to project into Germany as a powerful force abroad, will already become obsolete when they come into operation, according to Die Zeit.
The unfortunate frigate F125 was already in the news last year, when the German Navy rejected the ship and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It was the first time the Navy had returned a ship to the builder once handed over.
Few details are known of why it has been returned, but the German media have cited several “material and technical defects” of the ship that made it useless if it were used in combat missions.
Technical problems are important, as approximately 120 sailors, half of the crew needed for former Bremen-class frigates, operate on Baden-Württemberg-class ships.
In addition, it has also been known that the weight of the ship is excessive, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy's ability to add future improvements.
Rheinland-Pfalz is thought to weigh 7,000 tons twice as much as similar-class ships used by Germans during World War II.
In addition to defective materials, the cost of the project, which includes crew training, is also starting to pose a problem.
It is believed to have reached outstanding €3.1 billion ($3.6 billion) from an initial budget of €2.2 billion.
The problems of the new frigates have become a matter of special importance, if we take into account the recent warnings indicating that Germany's maritime military power is being weakened.
This year Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the defense committee of the German Parliament, acknowledged that the Navy “is running out of ships that can be used.”
The officer has stated that the situation has worsened over time because the old ships stopped being used, but none of them were provided as a replacement.
He lamented that none of the Baden-Württemberg class frigates could join the Navy.
The National Foundation spies the secret life of bats
New research carried out on an estate in the Scottish Highlands aims to uncover how bats use the landscape to feed themselves.
Discoveries are expected to provide new data on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and assist in future conservation activities.
The study by scientists at the National Foundation of Scotland will follow soprano and common bats, as well as northern orellut and water bats at the Inverewe Botanical Garden, located in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in key locations of the property to keep track of the activities of the bats during the station.
Volunteers and staff from the UK National Health Service will also perform mobile surveys with portable detectors.
The analysis of the sound of the recordings by experts will determine the sound frequency of the bats and what each species does.
With this information, a report and a habitat map will be created to create a detailed landscape-scale image of their behaviour.
Rob Dewar, natural conservation adviser at the National Foundation of Scotland, expects the results to show which areas of the habitat are most important for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will help determine the advantages of habitat management work, such as the creation of meadows, and how to improve the maintenance of forests for bats and other associated species.
Bat population in Scotland and the rest of the United Kingdom has fallen sharply in the last century.
The tasks of construction and restoration of buildings threaten bats, as they affect nests and can cause habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting also pose a risk, as does flick wallpaper and chemical treatments used in building materials, as well as domestic cat attacks.
Actually, bats aren't blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when they have to catch a prey.
They use an eco-locating sophistic technique to place insects and obstacles that stand in their flight.
The Scottish National Foundation, responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 major gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes bats very seriously.
It has ten experts with the appropriate training, who carry out surveys, nest inspections and, sometimes, rescues on a regular basis.
Even the organisation has created Scotland's first and only reserve dedicated to bats on the Threave Estate estate, located in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland's ten species of bat.
The administrator of the farm, David Thompson, states that it is the ideal territory for the species.
"Here in Threave we have a large area for bats," he says.
“We have old buildings, many old trees and a good habitat.
But we still don’t know many aspects of bats, so the work we do here and other properties will help us better understand what they need to grow.”
He emphasizes the importance of checking if there are bats before carrying out maintenance tasks on properties, since it may be that the possible involuntary destruction of a single maternity nest kills more than 400 females and young people, which could lead to the disappearance of an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill them, attack them, annoy them and destroy their nests.
Elisabeth Ferrell, an officer at the Scottish Foundation for the Conservation of Bats, has encouraged people to take a step forward to help.
“We still have to learn a lot from bats and, in the case of some of our species, we don’t have enough data about their population.”
Ronaldo denies rape allegations as lawyers prepare lawsuit against German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has indicated that accusations of rape against him are “false news” and has stated that people “want to become famous” thanks to his name.
His lawyers will sue the German weekly magazine Der Spiegel, responsible for publishing the accusations.
Portugal striker and Juventus have been accused of raping an U.S. woman, Kathryn Mayorga, in a Las Vegas hotel room in 2009.
Allegedly, he paid at the time $375,000 so that the incident would not be made public, according to information published Friday by Der Spiegel.
In an Instagram Live video addressing his 142 million followers hours after the allegations were made public, Ronaldo, 33, claimed they were “false news.”
"No, no, no, no.
“What they’ve said, fake news today,” says the five-time winner of the Golden Ball on camera.
“They want to become famous because of my name.
It's normal.
They want to take advantage of my name to become famous, but it's part of my work.
I'm a happy man and everything is in order," the player added, smiling.
Ronaldo's lawyers are already preparing the lawsuit against Der Spiegel over the accusations, which they have described as "inadmissible information of suspicions about private life," according to Reuters.
Christian Schertz said the player will seek compensation for “moral damages for an amount corresponding to the severity of the offence, which is probably one of the most flagrant infringements of personal rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident took place in June 2009 in a suite at the Palms hotel and casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga returned to the player's room, where he allegedly raped her annally, according to reports filed in the Court in Clark County, Nevada.
Mayorga claims Ronaldo knelt after the alleged incident and told him he was a “99% good guy,” except a “1%.”
Documents contend that Ronaldo confirmed that they both had sex, but were consensual.
Mayorga also claims that she went to the police and took photographs of the injuries in the hospital, but later accepted an agreement outside the courts because she was “fear of reprisals” and worried about “being publicly humiliated.”
The 34-year-old woman claims she seeks to overturn the deal, as she continues to be traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was about to sign for Real Madrid coming from Manchester United at the time of the alleged incident and this summer he has signed for Juve, the Italian giant, in an operation of 100 million euros.
Brexit: The UK “would always repent” of losing car manufacturers
The U.K. “would always regret it”, if it lost the state of the world leader in manufacturing after Brexit, according to Greg Clark, Secretary of Economic Affairs.
He added that it was “worrying” that Toyota’s base in the United Kingdom has told the BBC that, if the United Kingdom left the European Union without reaching any agreement, it would temporarily stop production at Burnaston factory, near Derby.
"We need an agreement," Clark said.
The Japanese manufacturer has indicated that the impact of border delays in the event of a no-deal Brexit could cause job losses.
The Burnaston plant, which manufactures Toyota's Auris and Avensis models, produced 150,000 cars last year, of which 90% was exported to the rest of the European Union.
“My view is that if the UK leaves the EU without agreement at the end of March, production will stop at our factory,” said Marvin Cooke, Toyota’s management director at Burnaston.
Leaving the EU without any deal on how trade will work with the rest of the EU countries has aroused the fears of other UK automakers, such as Honda, BMW, Jaguar and Land Rover.
For example, BMW claims it will close the Mini plant in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns are related to what manufacturers consider risks in the production chain in the event of no-deal Brexit.
Toyota’s production line is carried out on a “faith-on-time” base, with parts that arrive every 37 minutes from suppliers in the United Kingdom and the European Union for car manufacturing.
If the United Kingdom leaves the European Union without reaching an agreement on March 29, there may be unrest at the border, which the sector believes could generate delays and shortages of garments.
It would be impossible for Toyota to retain inventory for more than a day at Derbyshire plant, according to the company, and production should be halted as a result.
Clark claims Theresa May's Chequers plan for future relations with the EU is "calibrated accurately to avoid these border checks."
“We have to reach an agreement. We want to get the best agreement that allows us, as I say, not only to enjoy current success, but also to take advantage of this opportunity,” he said on BBC Radio 4’s Today programme.
“Tests not only of Toyota, but also of other manufacturers, indicate that we need to be able to continue with a successful set of production chains.”
Toyota has not been able to confirm for how long production would stop, but, in the long term, it has warned that added costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and could cause job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked at Burnaston for 24 years and is the coordinator of Unite the Union at the plant, said that members begin to be very concerned about the situation: “By experience, when these jobs are lost, they never return.”
“We have designed an accurate and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU.”
According to the White House, the meeting between Trump and Rosenstein could become delayed again
Donald Trump’s long-awaited meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be “postponed for a week” while continuing the fight over the supreme court nominee, Brett Kavanaugh, in statements from the White House on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of special adviser Robert Mueller, who investigates Russian interference in elections, links between Russia and aid to Trump and possible obstruction of justice by the president.
Whether or not Trump lays down the Deputy Prosecutor-General and jeopardize Mueller's independence has fueled rumors in Washington for months.
This month, the New York Times already reported that Rosenstein discussed the possibility of carrying a microphone to record the conversations with Trump and also the possibility of deposing the president with the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein has denied the information.
But last Monday he went to the White House among rumors that he was about to resign.
However, a meeting with Trump, who was at the United Nations in New York, was announced on Thursday.
Trump has stated that he "would prefer not" to fire Rosenstein, but the meeting was postponed to avoid a confrontation with the hearing of the Senate judicial committee testified by Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual misconduct, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-long investigation into the FBI over allegations against Kavanaugh, which further delayed the Senate's vote.
Trump's press secretary Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News Sunday.
Asked about the meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: "No date has yet been set, it could be this week, although it may be delayed one more week for all matters with the supreme court.
But we’ll see. I always like to keep the press informed.”
Some journalists could refute this claim: Sanders hasn't held any White House briefings since September 10.
Presenter Chris Wallace asked why.
Sanders replied that the lack of information sessions wasn’t because he didn’t like the bragging of television journalists, although he said, “I won’t deny that they’re bragging.”
He then hinted that direct contact between Trump and the press would be increased.
"The president gives more questions and answers sessions than any previous president," he said, before adding the following without citing any sources: "We've consulted this data."
Sanders has stated that briefings will continue to take place, but “if the press has the possibility to ask the President of the United States directly, it is very infinitely better than talking to me.
“We’ve been trying to do this often, you’ve seen us do a lot in recent weeks and they’ll replace information sessions when you can talk to the President of the United States.”
Trump answers questions when leaving the White House or participating in open sessions or press conferences with dignitaries on an official visit.
Individual press conferences are uncommon.
This week, in New York, the president has perhaps shown why, after an informal appearance and, at some point, strange in front of gathered journalists.
The Health Secretary writes to EU workers of the Scottish National Health Service in the wake of fears for Brexit
The Health Secretary has written to EU staff working at the Scottish National Health Service to express the country's gratitude and wishes for them to continue after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, sent a letter less than six months from the UK's departure from the EU.
The Scottish government has already pledged to cover the cost of residence status applications for EU citizens working in delegated public services.
"During the summer, negotiations between the United Kingdom and the European Union on leaving have continued, and everything seems directed towards the expected decisions this autumn.
But the UK government has also begun preparations in the event of a possible no-deal scenario.
I know it's turbulent times for all of you.
For this reason, I wanted to reiterate now that I greatly appreciate the contribution of all staff members, regardless of their nationality.
EU colleagues, and from beyond, provide valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of the health service, while benefiting patients and the communities we treat.
Of course Scotland is your home and we want you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie undergoes an emergency operation after suffering head injury
Christion Abercrombie, behind-the-ground Tennessee State Tigers has undergone an emergency operation after suffering a head injury Saturday in the 31-27 defeat against Vanderbilt Commodores, according to Mike Organ, from the Tennessean.
Tennessee's coach, Rod Reed, has told reporters that the injury occurred shortly before half-time.
"He came to the sideline and collapsed there," Reed said.
Trainers and medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen to the band before placing it on a stretcher and taking it away in order to do more tests.
A Tennessee State officer has reported Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee, that Abercrombie had left the operating room at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that "there's still no information about the type or extent of the injury" and Tennessee State is investigating when the injury occurred.
It's Abercrombie's second-year season with Tennessee State after signing from Illinois.
He had completed five tackles Saturday before leaving the game, making a total of 18 this season.
Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher doorbell tax when buying a property in the UK
Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher timbre tax when buying a property in the UK with the additional money used to help homeless people according to new Conservative Party plans.
This action will neutralize the success of Corbyn's movement to attract young voters.
The increase in stamp duty will be taxed on those people who don't pay taxes in the UK.
Hacienda expects to get up to £120 million annually to help homeless people.
Foreign buyers are expected to be taxed a higher doorbell tax when buying a property in the UK and that money will be used to help homeless people, a measure that Theresa May will announce today.
This action is seen as an attempt to neutralize the success of Jeremy Corbyn's movement to attract young voters with promises to provide more affordable homes that would affect higher incomes.
Rise in timbre tax will be taxed on those people and businesses that do not pay taxes in the UK; this money will be allocated to the government's action to combat the figures of homeless people.
The overcharge, which will be added to today's doorbell tax, including the highest levels introduced two years ago in second homes and real estate speculation, could be up to 3%.
Hacienda expects to earn up to £120 million annually.
It is estimated that 13% of London's new-building properties are non-British residents, which increases prices and makes it difficult for novice buyers to make their way on the property scale.
Many well-to-do areas of the country, especially in the capital, have become “ghost cities” as a result of the large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new measure comes weeks after Boris Johnson has called for a reduction in stamp duty to help young people have their first home.
He accused large constructs of keeping property prices high by buying land quickly and not using it, and urged May to override quotas on affordable homes to correct Britain’s “real estate disaster.”
Corbyn has announced a series of attractive proposed real estate reforms, such as controls on the rental and completion of evictions “without guilt”.
He also wants to give city councils more power to build new homes.
“Last year I said that I would dedicate my mandate to restoring the British dream: life has to improve for every new generation.
And that means correcting our broken real estate market.
Britain will always be open to anyone who wants to live, work and create a life here.
However, it cannot be correct that it is equally easy for people who do not live in the United Kingdom, as well as for overseas-based companies, to buy housing compared to hard-working British inhabitants.
For too many people, the dream of owning a home is too distant and the indignity of homeless people makes everything even more real.”
Jack Ross: “My greatest ambition is coaching Scotland”
Sunderland manager Jack Ross has stated that his “maximum ambition” is to be Scotland’s coach at some point.
The 42-year-old Scotsman has accepted the challenge of reliving the north-east club, which currently ranks third in League One, three points behind the leader.
He came to the Stadium of Light this summer after returning the St Mirren to the Scottish Premiership last season.
"I wanted to play for my country when I was a player.
I was once summoned as support and that's it," Ross said on BBC Scotland's Sportsound programme.
“But I grew up watching Scotland play in Hampden with my dad when I was a kid and it’s always something that makes me go back in time.
This opportunity will only come to me if I have success training at clubs, however.”
Ross' predecessors in Sunderland's coaching position include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic manager claims he doesn't feel the pressure to be next on a list of consecrated names on such a great team, having previously rejected Barnsley and Ipswich Town deals.
“At the moment, my criterion for measuring my success will be if I can bring this team back to the Premier League.
With the structure and facilities of the club, it is clear that its place is the Premier League, "he said.
"It's not easy to get there, but I'd only think I've been successful here if I can bring the club back."
Ross has only three years of experience as a coach, after a period as second coach at Dumbarton and 15 months at the Hearts coaching staff.
He helped Alloa avoid relegation to the third category and transformed St. Mirren from fighting for salvation to Championship champions the following season.
Ross states that he feels more comfortable now than during his time as a player at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St. Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It was probably a decisive moment," he recalled about accepting the position at the Alloa.
“I had always believed that training was what gave me the best, rather than playing.
It sounds strange because I did it right, I got away with it reasonably and I enjoyed enough good peak moments.
But playing can be very hard.
There's a lot you have to overcome every week.
I'm still going through it in aspects such as stress and pressure from work, but I feel good as a coach.
I’ve always wanted to train and now I’m doing it, it’s the most comfortable time I’ve felt like I’ve been an adult.”
You can listen to the full interview at Sportsound on Sunday, September 30 at Radio Scotland, between 12:00 and 13:00, UK time
The perfect time for a comb is 17:30 on a Saturday, according to a study
The summery heatwave has skyrocketed consumption in Britain's struggling bars, but added more pressure on restaurant chains.
Bars and bar chains have increased by 2.7% in sales in July, but consumption in restaurants has fallen by 4.8%, according to statistics.
Peter Martin, of the CGA financial consultancy, the company that collected the data, states the following: “The continuous hours of sunshine and England’s participation in the World Cup, which has lasted longer than expected, have meant that July follows a pattern similar to that of June, with an increase of 2.8% in bars, except in the case of restaurants, which have suffered even more.
Reduction of 1.8% in consumption in restaurants in June has worsened in July.
Bars and taverns, led by the consumption of drinks, have obtained the best results, while consumption in restaurants has gone down.
The bars that offer food have also suffered with the sun, although it has not been as dramatic as in the case of restaurants.
It seems that people just wanted to go out and drink.
In the bars and taverns that we have consulted, beverage sales have increased by 6.6% during the month, while food sales have been reduced by 3%.”
Paul Newman, RSM leisure and hospitality analysts, said: “These results continue the trend we’ve seen since the end of April.
Time and the impact of large social or sports events continue to be the main factors related to sales of the external leisure market.
It is no surprise that restaurants continue to have problems, but a decrease in sales of 4.8% year-on-year would be especially harmful, rather than continuous pressure for costs.
This long and hot summer could not have come at a worse time for shops that serve food and the weather will tell if the more moderate temperatures we have had in August will provide that much-needed respite.”
The total increase in sales of bars and restaurants, including those recently opened, has been 2.7% in July, data that indicates the slowdown in the presentation of brands.
The Coffer Peach Tracker sector sales indicator for UK restaurants, bars and taverns collects and analyzes performance data from 47 operator groups, with a combined loss of more than £9million and is the sector's established benchmark.
One in five children have secret social media accounts that hide parents
One in five children, some 11-year-old, have secret social media accounts hiding parents and teachers, according to data from a survey
A survey among 20,000 high school students has indicated an increase in the “false pages of Insta”
The news has increased fears about the publication of sexual content
20% of students say they have a “principal” account shown to parents
One in five children, some 11-year-old, create social media accounts that hide adults.
A survey among 20,000 high school students has indicated a rapid increase in Insta’s “false” accounts, a reference on Instagram, the place to share photos.
The news has increased fears about the publication of sexual content.
20% of students say that they have a net “principal” account shown to parents, while they also have a private one.
A mother who uncovered her 13-year-old daughter's secret account saw a message from a teenage girl urging others to "rape me."
The research, carried out by Digital Awareness UK and the Committee of Directors of Private Schools (HMC) of independent schools, has revealed that 40% of adolescents between the ages of 11 and 18 have two profiles, of which half have admitted that they have private accounts.
Mike Buchanan said: “It’s disturbing that many teenagers are tempted to create online spaces where parents and teachers can’t find them.”
Eilidh Doyle will be “athletes’ voices” on the Scottish Athletics Federation’s directive
Eilidh Doyle has been appointed non-executive director of the Scottish athletics federation directive at the organization's annual general meeting.
Doyle is the field and track athlete with the most medals from Scotland and President Ian Beattie described the movement as a great opportunity for those guiding the sport to benefit from his dilated international experience over the past decade.
"Eilidh has the respect of the athletics community in Scotland, the United Kingdom and around the world and we are sure that Scottish athletics will benefit from joining the directive," Beattie said.
Doyle said: “I’m really looking forward to acting as an athlete’s voice and I hope I’ll be able to grow sport and contribute to Scotland.”
The American, who won gold in the 200s and 400 at the 1996 Olympics in Atlanta, among a total of four Olympic golds and is currently a regular commentator on the BBC, was prevented from walking after suffering a transient ischemic attack.
He wrote on Twitter: “A month ago I suffered a stroke.
I couldn't walk.
Doctors told me that only time will tell if I can recover or to what degree.
It has been exhausting, but I have recovered completely, I have returned to learning to walk and today it's time to do agility exercises!
Thank you for the messages of encouragement!”
An advertisement for tirallets that compares mothers with cows divides opinion on the internet
A tyrallet company has divided the opinion on the Internet with an advertisement that compares breastfeeding mothers with cows that are milked.
To mark the launch of what is considered the “first silent thug you can wear in the world”, consumer technology company Elvie has issued an advertisement inspired by a joking music video to show the freedom given by the new tyrant to mothers.
Four real mothers dance in a barn full of fodder and cows to the rhythm of a song that includes lyrics such as: “Yes, I mumble myself, but you won’t see any queues” and “In case you hadn’t noticed, they’re not braguers, but my breasts.”
The return continues: “Bump, pump, feed the babies, pumping, pumping, pumping, bruising the girls.”
However, the advertisement, published on the company's Facebook page, has provoked controversy on the Internet.
With 77,000 views and hundreds of comments, the video has received a mixture of reactions from those who have seen it, of which many say it shows the “horrors” of the dairy industry.
“Very bad decision to use cows to advertise the product.
Like us, they have to get pregnant and give birth to produce milk, with the difference that they are separated from their babies a few days after giving birth," one person wrote.
The Elvie tyrant is discreetly hidden in a breastfeeding bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another person commented: “Comprehensibly traumatic for mother and baby.
But why not use them to advertise a strip for mothers who will keep their babies?”
Another user added: "It's a misplaced ad."
Other people defended the announcement; a woman admitted that she found that the song was “fun.”
"I think it's a great idea.
I'd buy one if I still had to breastfeed.
Pumping for the tirallet made me feel exactly as if it were a cow.
The announcement is a little crazy, but it has to be taken as what it is.
It's a great product," wrote one user.
Another person commented: “It’s a funny advertisement intended for mothers who pump with strips (usually in their workplace or in the toilet and feel like they’re cows.
“It’s not an announcement to praise or judge the dairy industry.”
At the end of the video, the women show that they have all danced with the tyrant discreetly placed on the bras.
The concept that is hidden behind the campaign is based on the perception said by many women who feel as if they were cows when they use the tirallet .
However, the Elvie tyrant is completely silent, has no cable or tube and discreetly fits into a breastfeeding bra, which gives women more freedom to move, hold babies and even go out while pumping.
Ana Balarin, Mother’s partner and early childhood developer, said: “The Elvie tirallet is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a daring and provocative campaign.
When drawing the parallel between women and cows, we wanted to show the public how to use a zipper and the challenges of doing so, while demonstrating, in an entertaining and didactic way, the incredible feeling of freedom offered by the new zipper.
It's not the first time Elvie tirallet has been on the news.
During London Fashion Week, a two-child mother appeared on the catwalk for designer Marta Jakubowski while using the product.
Hundreds of emigrant children silently transferred to tent camp on Texas border
The number of emigrant children retained has risen even though monthly entries across the border have practically remained stable, in part because the harsh rhetoric and policies introduced by the Trump administration have hindered the sponsorship of children.
Traditionally, most godparents were undocumented immigrants, who have been afraid to put at risk their chance of staying in the country if they decided to welcome a child.
Risk has risen in June, when federal authorities announced that potential godparents and other adults living in their homes will have to send fingerprints, which will be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week, Matthew Albence, a high-ranking officer in the Immigration and Customs Control Service, testified in front of Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people who had applied to host unaccompanied minors.
The agency later confirmed that 70% of those arrested had no previous criminal record.
“About 80% of the people who have welcomed or lived in the home of a person they have received are in the country illegally, and a large majority of them are criminal foreigners.
Therefore, we will continue to pursue these people," Albence said.
To prosecute children more quickly, officers have introduced new rules that require that some have to appear in court in a month after being held, rather than 60 days, which was the previous standard, according to shelter workers.
Many of them will appear by videoconference, rather than in person, to defend their case to obtain legal status before an immigration judge.
All those who are not considered suitable for help, will be deported immediately.
The more time children spend in custody, the more likely they will suffer from anxiety or depression, which can lead to violent outbursts or escape attempts, according to the workers and reports that the system has unveiled in recent months.
Lawyers claim that these concerns can intensify at larger facilities, such as Tornillo, where it is more likely that, due to their size, they will overlook the signs that a child is suffering.
They have added that moving the children to the camp without having time to prepare them emotionally or saying goodbye to friends can cause them trauma that many already suffer.
Syria urges “occupation forces” of U.S
Addressing the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Muallem called on Syrian refugees to return home, even though the war in the country has already lasted eight years.
Muallem, who also acts as deputy prime minister, has stated that foreign forces were on Syrian territory illegally, under the pretext of combating terrorism, and “the matter will be dealt with accordingly.”
"They must be withdrawn immediately and unconditionally," he told the assembly.
Muallem has insisted that the “war of terror has practically ended” in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have died since 2011 and the conflict has caused millions of displaced people.
Damascus would continue "fighting this sacred battle until we have purged the entire Syrian territory" of both terrorist groups and "any illegal foreign presence."
The U.S. has about 2000 troops in Syria, primarily to train and advise Kurdish and Syrian Arab forces opposing President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1000 troops on the ground of a country hit by the war.
On the issue of refugees, Muallem has stated that the conditions were good for his return and has blamed “some Western countries” for spreading “irrational fears” that make refugees want to stay away.
"We have called on the international community and humanitarian organizations to facilitate these returns," he said.
"They're politicizing an issue that should be purely humanitarian."
The U.S. and European Union have warned that there will be no aid to reconstruction for Syria until a political deal between Assad and the opposition to end the war.
United Nations diplomats have stated that the recent Russia-Turkey agreement to create a neutral zone for the last great rebel stronghold, Idlib, has favored the opportunity to continue with political encounters.
Russia-Turkey deal has prevented a large-scale attack by Syrian forces with Russia's aid to the province, where three million people live.
However, Muallem has remarked that the agreement has “clear deadlines” and has expressed the hope that military action will face the jihadists, which include fighters from the Al-Nusra Front linked to al-Qaeda, who “will be eradicated”.
Staffan de Mistura, UN envoy, hopes to convene the first meetings of a new committee made up of members of the government and the opposition to begin to outline a post-war constitution and pave the way for elections.
Muallem explained the conditions of the Syrian government’s participation in the committee, whose work should be restricted to “reviewing the articles of the current constitution” and warned of possible interference.
Why will Trump win for the second time
According to this logic, Trump would win the election again in 2020 unless, as many Liberal spectators probably hope, the scandals and the process of dismissal ended his presidency prematurely.
What would undoubtedly be “the end of a more dramatic presidency in history.”
At the moment, there are no signs of fatigue in spectators.
Since 2014, the number of viewers in prime-time hours has more than doubled to 1.05 million on CNN and practically tripled to 1.6 million at MSNBC.
Fox News has an average of 2.4 million viewers in prime-wing hours, from 1.7 million four years ago, according to Nielsen, and “The Rachel Maddow Show”, from MSNBC, has led cable television audiences with up to 3.5 million viewers on the main informative nights.
“People have seen themselves dumped because they don’t understand it,” said Neal Baer, executive producer of the ABC series “Designated Survivor”, about a cabinet decreary that happens to be the president when an attack destroys the Capitol.
Nell Scovell, a veteran comedy writer and author of “Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys’ Club,” has another theory.
He remembers a taxi trip to Boston ahead of the 2016 election.
The taxi driver told him he'd vote Trump.
“Why?” he asked.
"He replied that because it made him laugh," Scovell told me.
There's an entertainment point in chaos.
Of course, unlike television programs, the screenplay coming out of Washington could determine the future of Roe v. Wade, whether immigrant families will be able to gather and the state of the world economy.
Disconnecting is a luxury that only the most privileged spectators can afford.
And yet, being an informed citizen is going further when you’re in the sixth hour of seeing a group of experts debating Bob Woodward’s use of sources “for private use” for his book “Fear,” Paul Manafort’s $15,000 ostrich skin jacket (“a piece of coarse clothing like his pride,” titled the Washington Post) and the consequences of the horrifying descriptions.
In my case, I won't see Super Mario the same again.
“Part of what makes you seem to be part of a reality TV show is that every night gives you something new,” says Brent Montgomery, chief executive of Wheelhouse Entertainment and creator of “Pawn Stars” about the changes of characters and script twists of Trump’s program (quarring with the NFL, praising Kim Jong-Un).
You can't afford to miss an episode because you're left behind.
When I saw Fleiss this week, there was a temperature of about 25 degrees and it was sunny outside the house he has on the north coast of Kauai, but he stayed locked looking at MSNBC while recording CNN.
He couldn't disengage from it, not with Brett Kavanaugh about to confront the Senate judicial committee and the future of the Supreme Court hanging by a thread.
"I remember when we did those crazy programs then and people said it was the beginning of the end of Western civilization," Fleiss told me.
“I thought it would be kind of joking, but it seems that they were right.”
Amy Chozick, a writer who mainly covers business, politics and media matters for the Times, is the author of the “Chasing Hillary” memoir.
External money flood mid-term election races in House of Representatives tighter
It is no surprise that there is a flood of cash in the 17th district of Pennsylvania, thanks to the realleaness of the congressional districts that has made two current members have to compete for the same square.
This recent reassignment of the suburban district of Pittsburg involves Democrative representative Conor Lamb, who obtained the spot in another district after a special election last spring.
Lamb confronts another present-day representative, Republican Keith Rothfus, who currently represents the former 12th Pennsylvanian district, coming into conflict with the new 17th district.
The maps were redrawn after the Supreme Court of Pennsylvania decreed in January that the former districts were unconstitutionally divided in favor of the Republicans.
The race for the new 17th district has triggered a financial war between the party's major financial organizations, the Democratic Campaign Committee for Congress (DCCC) and the National Republican Campaign Committee (NRCC).
Lamb made a name for himself in Pennsylvania after a tight victory in a well-followed special election held in March to represent Pennsylvania's 18th congressional district.
Republicans have held office for more than a decade and President Donald Trump won in the district by 20 points difference.
Political experts give Democrats a slight advantage.
U.S. imposes sanctions on the Savior for his support of China, but withdraw them
The diplomats observed that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing, with little response from Washington.
Trump held a tense meeting with President Juan Carlos Valera in June 2017 and had a hotel in Panama until his partners kicked out the Trump Organization's management team.
Officers of the State Department decided to withdraw the heads of American diplomatic missions in El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama due to “recentual decisions on the cessation of acknowledging Taiwan”, declared Heather Nauert, spokesman of the department, this month.
However, there was only the possibility of sanctioning El Salvador, which received in 2017 U.S. aid of 140 million dollars, according to estimates, for the control of drug trafficking, development and economic improvements.
The proposed sanctions, which include cuts in financial aid and restrictions on visas issued, would be detrimental to the Central American country and high death tolls from murder and unemployment.
With the passage of internal meetings, officers of both countries postponed a high-level meeting focused on security and economic prosperity to continue with a similar meeting held last year and which was seen as a step forward to prevent emigrants from heading to the United States.
But by mid-September, high-ranking officers of the administration clarified that they wanted the meeting to take place, which effectively ended with any probability of sanction for El Salvador.
Vice President Mike Pence has been chosen for the meeting, scheduled now for mid-October, as a sign of the importance that the administration attaches to it, according to diplomats.
The three U.S. envoys discreetly returned to his posts in El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republique without harsh messages or sanctions from Washington.
Bolton's White House spokesperson declined to comment on details of the debate described by the three officers, which include two diplomats, who agreed to debate internal deliberations from anonymity.
An external analyst, close to the administration and also from anonymity, corroborated their statements.
Study History
The next event to take place could be Special Prosecutor Robert Mueller's report on Trump's possible obstruction of justice, of which there is numerous evidence in public records.
Apparently, Mueller is also investigating whether Trump's campaign collaborated with Russia on his attack on the election.
If Congress changes hands, Trump could find himself being held accountable to the body, just when he prepares to do so in front of voters, and even before a jury of party members.
There are many circumstantial conditions, and I don't want to suggest that the fall of Trump, or any of his European namesakes, is inevitable.
There are decisions that must be made on both shores of the Atlantic that will affect how long the situation will last.
In 1933, the German officers were ready to make a coup d'état against Hitler, only if the Western bloc had resisted it and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and missed the opportunity to avoid all the years of terror that followed afterwards.
The course of history fluctuates on inflection points like this and the inexorable progress of democracy is accelerated or delayed.
The U.S. is facing several inflection points today.
What will happen if Trump lays off Rod Rosenstein, the deputy attorney general, the man who controls the fate of research on Mueller?
Rosenstein has been with water around his neck since this newspaper reported that, last year, he suggested secretly recording the president and speculated he wasn't suitable for office.
Rosenstein asserts that Times information is not accurate.
“How are we going to answer whether the FBI’s new investigation of Brett Kavanaugh is not complete or fair, or whether it is confirmed for the Supreme Court despite credible accusations of sexual abuse and false testimony?
And, especially, will we vote in the middle of the legislature a Congress that will make Trump responsible for it?
If we don't pass these tests, democracy will start to be in danger.
But I don't think we're going to fail, because I already learned the lesson in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that occupied the ambassador's house.
He survived, emigrated to the United States and, 60 years later, sent me to light Sabbath candles at that table with the swastika.
With this background, how can I not be optimistic for the future?”
Norman Eisen, an experienced researcher at the Brookings Institution, is the president of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and the author of “The Last Palace: Europe’s Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House”.
Graham Dorrans of Rangers, optimistic for the match against Rapid Vienna
Rangers receives Rapid Vienna on Thursday, aware that a win against the Austrians, after an impressive draw on Villarreal's field this month, would leave him in a good position to qualify for the Europa League's G group.
Knee injury prevented midfielder Graham Dorrans from playing the first few minutes of the season until 2–2 draw against Villarreal, but he believes Rangers can use this result as a springboard for great results.
"It was a positive point for us, because Villarreal is a good team," said the 31-year-old player.
“We played the game believing that we could get a valuable result and we came out with one point.
Maybe we could have gotten something more in the end, but, in general, probably the tie was a fair result.
They were better in the first half, but we were better than them in the second.
On Thursday, it's another great European night.
We hope we can stay with the three points, but it will be a difficult game because they got a good result in the last game, but I'm sure we'll give everything and get a good result with the support of the fans.
Last year was really hard, with the injuries I suffered and the changes at the club, but now it's a good place to be.
The team is well and the boys enjoy it, the workouts are good.
I hope that we can give it all, leave behind last season and be successful.”
Women lose their sleep because of fear of retirement savings
Although the participants in the survey had a clear idea of how they wanted to be taken care of, few people talked about it with the family.
Approximately half of respondents nationwide say they've talked to their partner about the long-term care cost.
Only 10% of respondents say they have talked to their children.
"People want a relative to take care of them, but they don't dare to consider it," Holly Snyder, vice president of the life insurance company Nationwide, said.
You have to start here.
Speak to your partner and your children: you can't prepare your family to take care of you if you don't communicate your wishes with enough anticipation.
Work with your advisor and your family to decide where and how to receive care, as these decisions can be a significant factor in determining the cost.
Include your financial advisor in the conversation: it can also help you determine how to pay these expenses.
Your financing options for your long-term care can include a traditional long-term care insurance policy, an effective-value hybrid life insurance policy that helps you cover expenses or own insurance with your own resources, if you have the money you need to do so.
Prepare your legal documents: cut the step to legal battles.
Seek a representative to be able to designate a trusted person who supervises your medical attention and makes sure that the professionals fulfill your wishes in case you cannot communicate.
Consider also the possibility of hiring a notarial power of attorney for your finances.
It is advisable to select a trusted person to make financial decisions for you and ensure that your bills are paid in case of disability.
Don’t forget the little details: Imagine that your elderly relative has a medical emergency and is on his way to the hospital.
Could you answer questions about medications and allergies?
Specifies these details in a written plan to be prepared.
Finance is not the only factor that must be determined, but also with which doctors you want to deal," Martin said.
"What are medicines?
Who will take care of the dog?
Have this plan ready.”
Man shot several times with compressed air shotgun in Ilfracombe
A man has received several shotguns from a shotgun of compressed air while he was returning home after going out at night.
The 40-year-old victim was in Ilfracombe's Oxford Grove area, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, abdomen and hand.
"The officers have described the shooting, which occurred around 2.30 a.m. (in British summer hours), as a "random act".
The victim did not see his attacker.
His life is not in danger due to his injuries and the police have made a call to find witnesses.
Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have died due to a strong earthquake and tsunami that shook the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, according to authorities. The number of deaths is expected to increase.
As communications are cut off, relief units have not been able to obtain information from Donggala Regency, an area north of Palu closer to the epicenter of the 7.5 magnitude earthquake.
In Palu, more than 16,000 people have been evacuated after the disaster.
Below are some important facts about Palu and Donggala, on the island of Celebes:
Palu is the capital of Celebes Central province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the western coast of Celebes Island, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami occurred.
Donggala is a regency that stretches along more than 300 km of coastline to the north-west of Celebes Island.
The regency, an administrative region less than one province, had a population of approximately 299,200 in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the pillars of the economy of Central Celebes province, especially in the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel mine exploitation is also important in the province, but is mainly concentrated in Morowali, on the coast at the other end of Celebes.
Palu and Donggala have suffered several tsunamis over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's disaster mitigation agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
In 1996 there was also a tsunami in western Donggala that killed nine people.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific fire belt and earthquakes usually occur frequently.
Listed below are the main earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004: On December 26, a large earthquake on the western coast of the Indonesian province of Ace in northern Sumatra caused a tsunami that affected 14 countries and killed 226,000 people on the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes affected the western coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Thousands of people died on Nias Island on the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A magnitude 6.8 earthquake struck southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, causing a tsunami that wrecked the southern coast and killed nearly 700 people.
2009: An earthquake with a magnitude of 7.6 rocked an octopus area in the city of Padang, the capital of Western Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people died.
2010: An earthquake with a magnitude of 7.5 shook one of Mentawai's islands, off the coast of Sumatra, and caused a tsunami of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A mild earthquake affected Pidie Jaya's regency in Aceh and caused destruction and panic, as the population recalled the devastation of the deadly 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
This time, there was no tsunami, but more than 100 people were killed due to the buildings that fell.
2018: There were a series of major earthquakes on Lombok's tourist Indonesian island that killed more than 500 people, mainly in the north of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily trapped.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on charges of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice-presidency candidate Sarah Palin, has been arrested on charges of assault.
Palin, a 29-year-old native of Wasilla, Alaska, was arrested as a suspect in domestic violence, interferencing with a report of domestic violence and arrest resistance, according to a report published Saturday by Alaska State Troopers.
According to the police report, when an acquaintance tried to call the police to report the alleged crimes, he pulled the phone.
Palin is detained at the Mat-Su provisional detention center, where he is held with an unsecured obligation of USD 500, according to KTUU.
He appeared in court on Saturday, when he pleaded "not guilty, for sure" when he was asked how he testified, according to that chain.
Palin faces three Class A fouls, which means he could go to jail for up to a year and have to pay a fine of up to USD 250,000.
He is also accused of a lack of class B, which can be punished with one day in prison and $ 2,000 fine.
It's not the first time that criminal charges have been recorded against Palin.
In December 2017 he was accused of assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
Her mother, Sarah Palin, called police to report the alleged attack.
Currently, the case is in the hands of Alaska's veteran court.
In January 2016 he was accused of domestic aggression, interference with the report of a crime of domestic violence and possession of a weapon in a state of drunkenness in relation to the incident.
His partner testified that he had punched him in the face.
In 2016, Sarah Palin was criticized by groups of veterans for associating her son's violent behaviour with post-traumatic stress disorder stemming from her service in Iraq.
Earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia: Hundreds killed
At least 384 people have been killed after an earthquake shaken the Indonesian island of Celebes on Friday.
The 7.5-magnitude earthquake caused a tsunami and destroyed thousands of homes.
Electricity and communications networks do not work and the death toll is expected to increase over the next few days.
The quake occurred right on the coast of Celebes Central, northeast of the Indonesian capital, Jakarta.
On social networks, videos circulate with the moment of impact.
Hundreds of people had gathered at a festival on the beach in the town of Palu when the tsunami reached the coast.
Federal prosecutors are calling for exceptional death penalty for a suspect in the New York terror attack
New York federal prosecutors are calling for the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the suspect in the New Yorky terrorist attack who killed eight people: an extraordinary sentence that has not been applied to the state for a federal crime since 1953.
Saipov, 30, allegedly used a Home Depot rental truck to carry out an attack on a bike lane on the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan and run over pedestrians and cyclists during his journey, in October.
In order to justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov killed the eight victims "intentionally" and that "intentionally" caused serious personal injuries, according to the notice of intention to ask for the death penalty filed in the southern district of New York.
Both offences could result in a death penalty, according to the court's document.
Weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury attacked Saipov with an indictment with 22 offenses that included eight accusations of murder for the benefit of organized crime, which federal prosecutors usually use in mafia cases, in addition to a crime of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
Prosecutors claimed the attack required considerable "planning and premeditation" and described Saipov's way of acting as "inhumane, cruel and depraved."
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and losses to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," according to the warning.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's been a decade since the last time the southern district of New York brought to trial a death penalty case.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was convicted of the murder of two drug traffickers, but was eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time a death sentence was executed in a New York federal case was in 1953 for Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple executed after being convicted of conspiracy to carry out espionage actions for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Both Rosenbergs died in the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to court documents, Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, did not prove repentance in the days and months following the attack.
The police explained that he told investigators that he felt good about what he had done.
According to indictment, Saipov explained to authorities that he was inspired to carry out the attack after watching ISIS videos on his phone.
According to the police, he also requested that the ISIS flag be displayed in his hospital room.
He has pleaded not guilty to the accusation of 22 offences.
David Patton, one of the public federal advocates representing Saipov, claimed they were "obviously disappointed" with the prosecution's decision.
"We consider that the decision to ask for the death penalty, instead of accepting a declaration of guilt and a life sentence without the possibility of freedom will only prolong the trauma of these events for all the people involved," Patton stated.
Saipov's defence team had previously asked prosecutors not to ask for the death penalty.
Member of the parliament of the conservative party states that NIGEL FARAGE should be put in charge of the Brexit negotiations
Nigel Farage promised to "mobilize the People's Troops" today during a protest at the Conservative party conference.
The former UKIP leader has stated that politicians must "feel pressure from the Eurosceptics" when one of Theresa May's own members of parliament has suggested that she should lead negotiations with the EU.
Non-charge Conservative MP Peter Bone told Birmingham's march that the UK would already be out of the EU if Mr Farage were Brexit's secretary.
But Ms. May's defiance of reconciling her deeply divided ranks has been highlighted when Conservatives in favor of the union have come forward in another anti-Brexit protest in La City.
The prime minister is struggling to retain her Chequers compromise plan between attacks by Brexit supporters, union supporters and the EU.
Allies insist that it will manage to reach an agreement with Brussels despite negative responses and put pressure on Eurosceptics and the Conservative party to choose between their package and chaos.
Mr Bone told at Solihull's Leave Means rally that he wanted to undo Chequers' engagement.
He suggested that Mr. Farage should have been appointed to the House of Lords and that he should have been given responsibility for negotiations with Brussels.
'If he'd been in charge, we'd already be away," he said.
Parliament member Wellingborough added: "I'm going to defend Brexit, but we have to undo Chequers' commitment."
To specify his opposition in the EU, he added: "We did not fight world wars to be servile.
We want to make our own laws in our own country."
Mr. Bone dismissed the suggestions stating that public opinion had changed since the 2016 vote: "The idea that the British population has changed their mentality and wants to stay is totally false."
Pro-Brexit Conservative supporter Andrea Jenkyns also attended the march and stated before reporters: "I'm just telling you, Prime Minister, to listen to people."
"Chequers' commitment is unpopular among the general public, the opposition will not vote in favor, it is unpopular within our party and our activists, who are the people who take to the streets and win an election.
Abandon Chequers' commitment and start listening."
In a message addressed to Ms May, she added: "Prime ministers keep their jobs when they keep their promises."
Mr Farage said that politicians at the rally should feel the pressure if they want to betray the decision made in the 2016 referendum.
"This, now, is a question of trust between us (people) and our political class," he said.
"They're trying to betray Brexit and today we're here to tell them we're not going to allow them."
In a message to the excited crowd, he added: "I want you to make the political class, about to betray Brexit, feel the pressure."
"We are mobilizing the people's troops of this country who gave us the victory with Brexit and we won't rest until we become an independent, autonomous and proud United Kingdom."
In the meantime, union supporters marched through Birmingham before holding a two-hour rally in the city centre.
A small number of activists waved banners on the Conservatives against Brexit following the group's launch this weekend.
The Conservative member of the House of Lords Lord Adonis laughed at the Conservatives because of the security issues they suffered with an application from the party at the beginning of the conference.
"These are people who tell us that they can have the necessary IT systems and all the technology for the Canada plus plus agreement, for the frictionless border, for free trade without borders in Ireland," he added.
"It's a complete farce.
There's no option for a good Brexit," he added.
Warren plans to examine the possibility of running as a presidential candidate
U.S. Senator Elizabeth Warren says she will examine the possibility of running as a presidential candidate after the November election.
The Boston Globe reports that on Saturday, the Massachusetts Democrat spoke about her future at an open meeting in the western part of Massachusetts.
Warren, a regular critic of President Donald Trump, is unveiling for re-election in November against GOP State Representative Geoff Diehl, who was deputy president of Trump's 2016 Massachusetts campaign.
It has been the center of speculation about the possibility of removing Trump in 2020.
The Saturday-afternoon event in Holyoke was his 36th reunion with constituents with the open meeting format since Trump took office.
An attendee asked him if he planned to run as a presidential candidate.
Warren replied that it's time "women go to Washington to repair our government that doesn't work, and that includes a woman in the dome."
Person arrested for Sims shot dead LSU
Baton Rouge's policing, La., announced on Saturday that a suspicion has been arrested for the shooting death of LSU Wayde Sims basketball player on Friday.
Baton Rouge's policing department has announced the arrest of Dyteon Simpson, 20, at the ET press conference at 11:00.
They posted a video of the shooting on Friday and asked for collaboration to identify man from the footage.
Sims, 20, received a shoot and died near the Southern University campus in the early hours of Friday.
"Wayde Sims was shot in the head that eventually killed him," police officer Murphy J. Paul commented Saturday in the media for 247sports.
Wayde tried to defend his friend and took a shot from Simpson.
Simpson was interrogated and claimed to have been at the scene, in possession of a weapon, and to have shot Wayde Sims.
Simpson was arrested without incident and was taken to custody at East Baton district police department.
Sims, a 2-metre junior player who grew up in Baton Rouge, played 32 games with 10 starts over the last season and averaged 17.4 minutes, 5.6 points and 2.9 rebounds per game.
Russian Grand Prix: Lewis Hamilton wins the world title after the team's orders handed him victory over Sebastian Vettel
From the moment Valtteri Bottas came ahead of Lewis Hamilton on Saturday, it became clear that the orders of the Mercedes team would play an important role in the race.
From pole position, Bottas had a good start and almost exhausted Hamilton while defending his position in the first two laps and invited Vettel to attack his partner.
Vettel went into pits earlier and let Hamilton run with the traffic in the back of the package, which should have been decisive.
Later, Mercedes had to stop in pits and came out behind Vettel, but Hamilton overtake after some wheeled action that prompted the Ferrari driver to leave the inside free with the risk of being left out after a double move to defend the third corner.
Max Verstappen started in the last row of the grid and at the end of the first lap he was in the seventh position on his 21st birthday.
He then continued in good position much of the race as he retained his wheels to save time, overtaking Kimi Raikkonen and moving to fourth place.
He finally entered the pits on lap number 44, but could not increase the pace at the next eight laps and Raikkonen was in fourth position.
It's a difficult day, because Valtteri did a fantastic job throughout the weekend and he was a real gentleman who was told to let me through.
The team has done a fantastic job to have a one-two," Hamilton said.
His bodily language was terrible
President Donald Trump laughed at Senator Dianne Feinstein at a rally on Saturday to insist that she did not leak Christine Blasey Ford's letter in which she accused Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh of sexual abuse.
At a rally in West Virginia, the president did not speak directly of Ford's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee, but commented that what was happening in the Senate showed that people are "bad, cruel and liared."
"Something that could happen and the most beautiful thing about what's being going on in recent days in the Senate, when you see anger, when you see angry, bad, cruel and lying people," she said.
"When you look at the communiqués and the leaks and say "No, it wasn't me.
It wasn't me.'
Do you remember?
Dianne Feinstein, did you filter it?
Remember your answer... Did you filter the document? "Huh, what?
No, no.
"I didn't filter it."
Hey, wait a minute.
We filtered... No, we didn't leak it," she added while imitating the senator.
Feinstein received the letter detailing Ford's statements against Kavanaugh in July and leaked in September, but Feinstein denied that the leak came from his office.
"I didn't hide Dr Ford's remarks, I didn't leak her story," Feinstein explained to the committee.
"He asked me to be confidential and I kept it confidential, just as he asked me."
But his denial didn't please the President, who commented at Saturday night rally: "Know what? His bodily language was terrible.
Maybe he didn't, but it was the worst bodily language I've ever seen."
Continuing his defense of the Supreme Court candidate, accused of inappropriate sexual conduct by three women, the president suggested that Democrats were using statements for their own purpose.
"They are determined to regain power by the necessary means.
You see wickedness, cruelty doesn't care about those who hurt, who they have to run over to have power and control," the President said.
Elite League: Dundee Stars 5-3 Belfast Giants
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
Dundee Stars repaired Friday's Elite defeat against Belfast Giants as they won the return match 5 to 3 at Dundee on Saturday.
The Giants immediately got a two-goal advantage thanks to the finishes of Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie climbed their team's level, but then Dwyer restored the Giants advantage.
Francois Bouchard equalized the Dundee. Afterwards, two goals from Lukas Lundvald Nielsen secured their victory.
It was the third defeat of the season in the Elite League for Adam Keefe's men, who came from beating Dundee 2-1 in Belfast on Friday.
It was the fourth match of the season between the two teams and the Giants had won all three games earlier.
Dwyer's first goal came from Dwyer in the fourth minute, at 3:35, from an assist from Kendall McFaull. Four minutes later, with the assistance of David Rutherford, Beauvillier would double his lead.
During this hectic initial period, Sullivan re-placed the home team in the game in minute 13:10 before Matt Marquardt facilitated the equalizer at minute 15:16.
Dwyer guaranteed the Giants advantage in the first break when he got the second goal of the night at the end of the first period.
The home team regrouped and, again, Bouchard contributed to his level with a powerful goal in the 27:37 minute.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran combined to help Nielsen give Dundee the advantage for the first time during the game at the end of the second half and guaranteed his team's victory with the fifth goal close to the end of the game.
The Giants, who have already lost four of the last five games, are at home, to Milton Keynes and have their next game on Friday.
An air traffic controller dies to save hundreds of passengers from an earthquake plane
An Indonesian air traffic controller has become a hero after he died to make sure that a plane with hundreds of passengers landed safely.
More than 800 people have died and many have disappeared after a major quake shaken the island of Celebes on Friday and triggered a tsunami.
Many aftershocks are taking place in the area and there are many people trapped among the debris in the city of Palu.
But, even though his comrades escaped to save their lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to quit his place in the trembling control tower at Palu Mutiara Sis Al Jufri Airport.
He stayed in place to make sure the Batik 6321 flight, which was on the runway at the time, could take the flight safely.
Afterwards, he stepped out of the air traffic control tower when he thought it was sinking.
He later died in the hospital.
A representative of Air Navigation, Indonesia, Yohannes Sirait, said this decision could have saved hundreds of lives, according to Australia's ABC News.
We prepared a Balikpapan helicopter in Kalimantan to take him to a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, we lost it this morning before the helicopter arrived in Palu.
"That's broken our hearts," he added.
In the meantime, authorities fear that the death toll could reach thousands, while the disaster mitigation agency has announced that access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
"The death toll is expected to increase because there are still many bodies under the debris and there are still many that have not been able to be accessed," said Sutopo representative Purwo Nugroho.
The waves, with a height of up to six meters, have devastated Palu, which will hold a common funeral on Sunday.
Armed forces and some commercial aircraft are bringing aid and groceries.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: "Every minute, an ambulance is carrying corpses.
Drinking water is scarce.
All mini-markets have been looted."
Jan Gelfand, director of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: "The Indonesian Red Cross works against the clock to help survivors, but we don't know what they'll find.
That's already a tragedy, but it might get even worse."
Indonesian President Joko Widodo arrived in Palu on Sunday and told the armed forces: "I ask everyone that you work day and night to complete all tasks related to the evacuation.
"Are you ready," CNN reported.
Indonesia suffered another earthquake this year in Lombok, where more than 550 people were killed.
Micronesia aviation accident: Air Niugini now says a man has disappeared after the accident in the lagoon
The airline operating a flight that's going to crash into a Pacific lagoon in Micronesia now says a man has disappeared, even though they previously said the 47 passengers and crew had safely evacuated the sinking plane.
Air Niugini said in a statement that, since Saturday afternoon, they could not find a passenger.
The airline claimed to be working with local authorities, hospitals and investigators to try to find it.
The airline did not immediately respond to requests for additional details about the passenger, such as their age or nationality.
Local boats helped rescue other passengers and crew when the plane fell into the water while trying to land at Chuuk Islands airport.
Authorities reported on Friday that seven people had been taken to a hospital.
The airline said on Saturday there were still six passengers in the hospital and that they were all in good condition.
The reason for the accident and the exact sequence of events are still unknown.
Both the U.S. airline and Navy claimed that the plane soaked in the lagoon because the runway fell short.
Some witnesses think the plane overlooked the runway.
U.S. passenger Bill Jaynes explained that the plane was very low.
"That was extremely positive," Jaynes claimed.
Jaynes said that he himself and other passengers managed to walk with water by the waist to the emergency exits of the plane, which was sinking.
He said flight attendants panicked and started screaming, and that he suffered a slight wound to the head.
The U.S. Navy sailed sailors who were working nearby to improve a dock also assisted in the rescue with an inflatable boat to transport the people affected on land before the plane sank about 30 meters into the water.
Data from the Aviation Safety Network indicates that 111 people have died in PNG-registered airline accidents, but Air Niugini was not involved in any of these accidents.
Analyst composes a chronology of the night a woman burned alive
Prosecutors conclude the case on Saturday in the review of the trial of a man accused of burning a Mississippi woman alive in 2014.
Justice Department analyst Paul Rowlett testified for hours as an expert witness in the field of intelligence analysis.
He described to the jury how he used cellphone records to build the movements of the defendant, 29-year-old Quinton Tellis and 19-year-old victim Jessica Chambers, the night he died.
Rowlett said he received location data from different mobile phones showing that Tellis was with Chambers the night he died, which contradicted his previous statements, The Clarion Ledger reported.
When the data showed that his mobile phone was with Chambers at the time when he claimed he was with his friend Michael Sanford, police went to talk to Sanford.
On Saturday, Sanford denied it and testified that that day he was not in the city.
When prosecutors asked if Tellis was telling the truth when he claimed he was in the Sanford pickup truck that night, Sanford said he had lied, as his pickup truck was in Nashville."
Another incoherence was that Tellis claimed that he had known Chambers for about two weeks when he died.
Telephone records indicated that they had only been known for a weeh.
Rowlett said that, some time after Chambers' death, Tellis deleted the text messages, calls and contact information of Chambers from his phone.
"He erased it from his life," Hale said.
The defense plans to start its final arguments on Sunday.
The judge said he hoped that the trial would pass at the hands of the jury throughout the same day.
The High Breed: What is conscious hip-hop?
A hip-hop trio fills their music with positive messages to challenge the genre's negative view.
Bristol's The High Breed says hip-hoop has turned away from its origins with political messages and social problems.
They want to return to their roots and make conscious hip-hop fashionable again.
Some artists, such as The Fugees and Common, have experienced a recent resurgence in the United Kingdom from artists such as Akala and Lowkey.
Another black person?!
NY's babysitter sues couple for dismissal after 'racist' message
A nannier from New York has denounced a couple for a discriminatory dismissal after receiving an ill-directed text message from the mother protesting because she was "another black person."
The couple denies that they are racists and compare the lawsuit with an "extortion."
Lynsey Plasco-Flaxman, mother of two, expressed dismay when she saw that the new nanny, Giselle Maurice, was black when she came to work on the first day in 2016.
"NOOOOOOOOOO ANOTHER BLACK PERSON," Mrs Plasco-Flaxman wrote to her husband in a text message.
But, instead of sending it to her husband, she sent it to Mrs. Maurice, twice.
After realising his wife, an "uncomfortable" Mrs. Plasco-Flaxman fired Mrs. Maurice, claiming that the nanny she had fired, who was African-American, had done her job badly and that she waited for a Filipino person, according to the New York Post.
Mrs. Maurice received the payment corresponding to her working day and then sent her home with an Uber.
Now, Maurice claims the couple compensation for the dismissal and asks for compensation of approximately 350 USD a day for the six-month residency job for which he had been hired, even though there was no contract.
"I want to show them that you can't do things like that," he explained in Post Friday, "I know it's discrimination."
The couple have responded to statements that they are racists while claiming Maurice's dismissal was the most reasonable, as they were afraid of not being able to trust her after being offended.
"My wife sent him something he didn't want to say.
It's not racist.
We'ren't racist people," said husband Joel Plasco in the Post.
"But, would you leave your children in the hands of someone with whom you've been rude, even if it was by mistake?
A baby?
“Come on.”
Plasco, who has compared the demand with an "extortion", claimed that his wife had two months left to have a baby and that she was in a "very difficult situation."
"Are you going to chase something like that?
It's not a very kind attitude," the investor banker added.
The legal case is not yet resolved and the court of public opinion has rushed to denounce the couple on social networks and condemn their behavior and logic.
