Grief women hang cards to prevent suicides on a bridge
Two women who have lost loved ones who have committed suicide are working to prevent others from doing so.
Sharon Davis and Kelly Humphreys have been hanging cards on a Welsh bridge with inspiring messages and phone numbers that citizens can call to receive assistance.
Ms. Davis's son, Tyler, was 13 when he began suffering depression and, at the age of 18, was killed.
"I don't want any parents to feel what I have to feel every day," he said.
Mrs. Davis, 45, residing in Lydney, said her son was a promising chef with a contagious smile.
"Everyone knew him because of his smile.
They always said that their smile could enlighten any room."
However, he quit his job before he died because he was at a "very dark spot."
In 2014, Tyler's brother, who was 11 at the time, found him after he had taken off his own life.
Mrs. Davis stated, "I'm continually concerned that there's a collateral effect."
Mr. Davis created the cards "so that people know that there are people you can turn to, they can hear, even if it's a friend.
Don't stay silent: you have to talk."
Mrs. Humphreys, who has been friends with Mrs. Davies for years, lost Mark, her partner for 15 years, shortly after the death of Mark's mother.
"He never said he wasn't feeling well or that he was depressed or anything like that," he said.
"A couple of days before Christmas we observed a change in attitude.
He touched heavily on Christmas Day, when the children opened his gifts and he didn't look at them in the eyes or anything."
He said his death was a great trauma to them, but that they have to work it: "It opens a hole in the family.
