Brett Kavanaugh's future holds up for a thread while the FBI initiates an investigation
"I thought that if we could get something like what he demanded (an investigation with a scope and a limited time), we could achieve some unity," said Mr. Flake on Saturday. He also added that he feared that the committee would be destroyed with the deadlock of supporters.
Why didn't Mr. Kavanaugh and his Republican supporters want an FBI investigation?
Their reluctance has to do with the moment.
There are only five weeks left for the November 6 mid-term election. If, as expected, Republicans don't get good results, they'll see weaknesses in getting the man they want to be elected to reach the country's top court.
George W. Bush has been picking up the phone to call the senators and pressure them to support Mr. Kavanaugh, who has worked in the White House for Mr. Bush. He also met his wife Ashley through Mr. Bush as she was his personal secretary.
What will happen after the FBI creates its report?
There will be a vote in the Senate, where, currently, there are 51 Republicans and 49 Democrats.
It remains unclear whether Mr Kavanaugh can get at least 50 votes in the Senate, which would allow Mike Pence, the Vice-President, to undo the tie and confirm it to the Supreme Court.
North Korea's number of deserters "falls" with Kim
North Korea's number of deserters in South Korea has fallen since Kim Jong Un came to power seven years ago, according to a South Korean lawmaker.
Park Byeong-seug, citing data from the Ministry of Southern Unification said 1,127 defections had occurred last year, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Mr. Park said that the strict border controls between North Korea and China and the increase in prices charged by human traffickers had been the main factors.
Pyongyang has made no public comment.
Normally, the vast majority of deserters in the North are offered South Korean nationality.
Sources in Seoul claim that more than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most escape through China, which has a longer border with North Korea and is easier to create than the demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas.
China considers defectors illegal migrants, rather than refugees and often repatriates them by force.
Relations between the North and the South, which are technically still at war, have improved considerably in recent months.
At the beginning of the month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang to hold talks focused on stopped negotiations on denuclearization.
This happened after the historic June meeting between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, when they agreed in general terms to work to get a nuclear-free Korean peninsula.
But on Saturday, North Korea's foreign affairs minister, Ri Yong-ho, blamed U.S. sanctions for a lack of progress since then.
"With no confidence in the U.S., there will be no trust in our national security and, in these circumstances, we will not disarm ourselves first unilaterally," Mr. Ri said in a speech at the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi says Brett Kavanaugh is a "hysteric" and that he is unfit to work in the High Court
House minority leader Nancy Pelosi said High Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh is a "hysteric" and that her temperament incapacitates her to work in the High Court.
Pelosi made such comments in an interview Saturday at the Texas Tribune Festival held in Austin, Texas.
"I can't stop thinking that if a woman had acted this way, they would have told her it's a "hysteric," Pelosi said in relation to her reaction to Kavanaugh's testimony in front of the Senate Judiciary Committee on Thursday.
Kavanaugh, excited, denied allegations of having sexually abused Dr. Christine Blasey Ford when both were teenagers.
During his initial statement, Kavanaugh was very excited, sometimes screaming and being speechless as he talked about his family and his years in high school.
He also explicitly condemned committee Democrats and said accusations against him entailed a "grotesque and coordinated murder of a character" organized by angry liberals because Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi said Kavanaugh's testimony demonstrated that he could not work in the High Court as it showed his prejudices against Democrats.
"I think he has disqualified himself with these statements and the way he attacked the Clintons and Democrats," he said.
Pelosi showed reluctance when asked if he would try to challenge Kavanaugh's election, if it's confirmed, and if Democrats get a majority in the House of Representatives.
"That's what I can say: if he doesn't tell the truth to Congress or the FBI, he's not only unable to be in the High Court, but also in court where he's now," Pelosi commented.
Kavanaugh currently serves as a judge in the Court of Appeal for the District of Columbia Circuit.
Pelosi added that, as a Democrat, she was concerned about the decisions Kavanaugh could make against the Affordable Care Act or the Roe versus Wade case, as he is considered a conservative judge.
In his confirmation hearings, he dodged questions about whether he would overturn certain decisions by the High Court.
"This is not the time for a hysterical person with prejudice to enter the Court and wait for us to seem wonderful," Pelosi said.
Women have to exercise their power.
It's a justified diatribe: months and years of abundant fury and you can't take it out without crying.
"Let's cry when we get angry," Mrs. Steinem told me 45 years later.
"I don't think it's uncommon. And you?"
She continued... "I was very helped by a woman who was an executive somewhere who told me that she also cried when she was angry, but who developed a tactic that consisted of, when she was angry and starting to cry, telling the person she was talking to: "Maybe you think I'm sad because I'm crying.
I'm angry.'
And then it continued.
It seemed brilliant to me.”
Tears are allowed as an outlet for rage in part because, basically, they are misunderstood.
One of the clearest memories that I have of one of my first jobs in an office with a male majority, where I once ended up crying with an indescribable rage, is that of an older woman (a cold directive that had always terrified me a little) who grabbed me from the neck and dragged me to the eye of the ladder.
"Never let them see you crying," he told me.
'They don't know you're furious.
They think you're sad and they'll rejoice, because they're going to have you."
Patricia Schroeder, who was then a Democratic Colorado MP, had worked with Gary Hart as a presidential nominee.
In 1987, when Mr. Hart was discovered in an extra-marital adventure in a boat called Monkey Business and retired from the race, Mrs. Schroeder, very frustrated, thought that there was no reason not to explore the idea of presenting herself as a candidate for the presidency.
"It wasn't a well-meditated decision," he told me as he laughed 30 years later.
"There were already seven other career candidates and the last thing they needed was another.
Someone called it "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs"
As she was late for the campaign, she was delayed with fundraising, so she promised she wouldn't access the race if she didn't raise US$2 million.
It was a losing battle.
He discovered that some of her supporters, who gave $1,000 to men, were only willing to give 250 to her.
"Do you think I've got a discount?" he wondered.
When he gave his speech and announced that he would not start a formal campaign, he was so overwhelmed by emotions (gratitude for the people who had helped him, frustration with the system for the difficulty of raising money and for targeting voters, rather than doing so to delegates, and rage, and sexism) that he was speechless.
"You might have thought I had a nervous breakdown," Mrs. Schroeder recalled about the press reaction.
"You might have thought my corporate sponsor was Kleenex.
I remember thinking: What are they going to put in my grave?
"He cried"?"
How the U.S.-China trade war can benefit Beijing
Initial savings from the U.S.-China trade war were deafening and, although it doesn't seem like the war doesn't have to end soon, a distancing countries may be beneficial for Beijing in the long term, according to experts.
Donald Trump, the U.S. President, issued his first warning earlier this year, when he imposed taxes on China's major exports, such as solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation occurred this week, with new tariffs affecting products worth USD 200,000 million. These tariffs account for an imposition of taxes on half of all goods arriving in the U.S. from China.
Beijing has counterattacked each time in the same way and has recently rised tariffs of five to ten-percenture for USD $60,000 million of U.S. goods.
China has pledged to replicate the U.S
Making Washington back entails giving in to some demands, but bowing publicly to the U.S. would be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, China's president.
However, experts say that, if Beijing plays its cards well, pressures of the trade war with the U.S. could benefit China in the long term by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
"The fact that a quick political decision in Washington or Beijing could create the conditions that initiate a fall in any of these countries is much more dangerous than witnesses had anticipated," said Abigail Grace, an Asia-centered research associate at the Center for New American Security.
Syria is "prepared" for refugees to return, foreign minister
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and asks for help to rebuild the country, devastated by a war of more than seven years.
Ahead of the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said conditions in the country are improving.
"Currently, the situation on the street is more stable and safe, thanks to the progress that has been made in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate the areas that terrorists destroyed to restore normality.
All the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to the country who had to leave due to terrorism and unilateral economic measures that affected their daily lives and their maintenance are met.
The EU estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the beginning of the war in 2011.
There are also six million people living in the country and in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem stated that the Syrian regime appreciates any help in rebuilding the devastated country.
But he emphasized that he would not accept conditional aid or aid from countries that funded the insurgency.
The European national team wins the Ryder Cup in Paris
The European national team has won the 2018 Ryder Cup by defeating the American national team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National, on the outskirts of Paris, France.
The U.S. has already lost six consecutive times on European ground and have not won the Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
The European national team won the crown again when the team of Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's reached 14.5 points necessary to win the United States.
U.S. star Phil Mickelson, who struggled for most of the tournament, kicked into the water on the hole par 3 16, which yielded the victory to Francesco Molinari.
The Italian golfer Molinari shined in all his series and became one of the four players who have achieved the 5-0-0 since he started the current format of the tournament in 1979.
The American Jordan Spieth was separated with 5&4 by the less-qualified player of the European team Thorbjorn Olesen, from Denmark.
The world's most qualified player, Dustin Johnson, lost 2 and 1 to Ian Poulter, from England, who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
The veteran with eight Ryder Cups, Spaniard Sergio Garcia, became the biggest winner the tournament has ever had, with 25.5 race points.
'I don't usually cry, but I can't help it today.
It's been a tough year.
Thank you, Thomas, for choosing me and believing in me.
I'm so happy, so glad I recovered the cup.
The team is the most important thing... And I'm happy to have been able to help," commented an excited Garcia after the European victory.
He takes over to his compatriot John Ram, who defeated American golf legend Tiger Woods 2&1 in an individual competition on Sunday.
"I'm incredibly proud to have won Tiger Woods, I grew up following him," said Rahm, 23.
Woods lost all four matches he had in France and now has a career record in the Ryder Cup of 13-21-3.
A strange statistic for one of the best players that have existed, with 14 great titles won and only behind Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S team struggled all weekend to look for the streets, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who played high-class golf throughout the tournament.
U.S. captain Jim Furyk spoke after his team's frustrating performance: "I'm proud of those players, they've struggled.
This morning there has been a time when we have pressured the European national team.
We've struggled.
I'll take off my hat for Thomas.
He's a great captain.
His 12 players have played very well.
We will regroup, work with the PGA of America and our Ryder Cup committee and make progress.
I love those 12 players and I'm proud to have been their captain.
There's something to take off your hat.
They have defeated us.”
Red tide update: Concentrations are reduced in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission's latest report shows an overall reduction in red tidal concentrations in certain parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, more dispersed proliferation conditions are being reported in the areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, suggesting lower concentration.
Red tide proliferation extends along approximately 200 km of coastline, from northern Pinellas to the counties of southern Lee.
Spots can be found 16 km off Hillsborough county coast, but in fewer places, compared to last week's situation.
Red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Medium-discipline concentrations have been reported on the coast or off the coast in Pinellas County over the course of last week, high-distance concentrations off the coast in Hillsborough County, concentrations of funds or highs in Manatee County, middle-to-the-country indoor concentrations in Charlotte County, high-flood concentrations on the coast or distance from the Lee County coast, and Collier's low-county concentrations.
Respiratory irritations continue to be reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
No respiratory irritation has been reported over the course of last week in northeastern Florida.
